var e={notice:`The data here is/may be copyrighted and NOT included in the GPLv3 license. Converted from MegaMek's unit files (https://github.com/MegaMek/megamek): MegaMek Data (C) 2025-2026 by The MegaMek Team, licensed under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/).`,shared:[{manufacturer:`StarCorps Industries`,primaryFactory:`Emris IV`},`The Kopis is an assault-class battle armor suit developed by StarCorps Industries for the Free Worlds League. Entering service in 3077 during the final years of the Jihad, the suit was designed as a heavy urban combatant capable of sustained engagements without the ammunition constraints common to missile-armed assault suits. Built at StarCorps' Emris IV facility, the Kopis earned the nickname "The Dark Alley" for its lethality in confined urban terrain, where its heavy armament and thick armor protection more than compensated for limited ground speed. The design became a mainstay of Free Worlds League and Oriente Province battle armor formations.`,`A refinement of the AI Mk II, this variant downgrades the heavy recoilless rifles to medium recoilless rifles, freeing weight and volume for reactive armor plating. The reactive armor significantly improves survivability against missile and ballistic attacks, addressing one of the original Mk II's weaknesses against concentrated fire. Heavy grenade launchers and heavy battle claws carry forward from the Mk II, maintaining the configuration's ballistic-focused anti-personnel capability.

The shift to medium recoilless rifles reduces per-shot damage output but preserves the suit's ability to engage infantry and light vehicles at range. The addition of reactive armor was inspired by the successful integration of similar protection on the Xiphos battle armor, giving the Mk IIr greater staying power in prolonged engagements where sustained incoming fire would have degraded the original Mk II's combat effectiveness.`,`The Kopis emerged from a crisis in Free Worlds League battle armor production. When suspected Word of Blake operatives sabotaged Etna Foundries' Longinus factory on Oriente, Thomas Halas recognized the League's vulnerability and turned to StarCorps Industries on Emris IV. StarCorps had manufacturing infrastructure from its Gorilla industrial exoskeleton line but lacked military-grade powered armor expertise. Duchess Alys Rousset-Marik brokered a solution, leveraging her resistance network to recruit Dr. Hyram Sundahar, a scientist who had fled Blakist-controlled Irian BattleMechs. Armed with Blakist technical documents obtained through the resistance, Sundahar adapted an Irian assault suit prototype into what became the Kopis.

The first suits reached the field in late 3077, assigned to the Twelfth Atrean Dragoons under Rousset-Marik's command. They proved themselves during the liberation of Outreach, where eight Kopis suits ambushed a retreating Widowmaker company and destroyed eighty percent of the mercenary force at a cost of only three suits. The Kopis participated in the Liberation of Terra in significant numbers and became the backbone of Marik and Oriente battle armor formations in the postwar period. Oriente later leveraged its Kopis production as a diplomatic tool, trading suits to The Republic of the Sphere in exchange for access to Achileus and Phalanx designs.`,{la:{manipulator:`heavy-battle-claw`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1},ra:{manipulator:`heavy-battle-claw`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1}},{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-heavy-grenade-launcher`,`la`,`#0`],[`is-heavy-grenade-launcher`,`ra`,`#1`],[`is-medium-recoilless-rifle`,`body`,`#2`],[`is-medium-recoilless-rifle`,`body`,`#3`],[`is-improved-sensors`,`body`,`#4`]]},`Developed for the final Coalition assault on Terra, this configuration replaces the standard model's twin medium lasers with a medium pulse laser and a flamer. The pulse laser offers improved accuracy against personnel targets, while the flamer excels at clearing fortified positions and flushing defenders from buildings. The heavy battle claws and anti-personnel weapon mounts are retained from the base design.

This loadout sacrifices the sustained ranged firepower of the standard model in favor of close-quarters lethality against entrenched infantry. The suit retains the same advanced armor protection and ground speed profile as other Kopis configurations, making it equally dependent on VTOL transport or defensive positioning for effective deployment. Against fanatical defenders holding urban centers, the combination of area-denial flamer and precision pulse laser proved well suited to the brutal room-by-room fighting that characterized late-Jihad clearance operations.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-medium-pulse-laser`,`body`,`#0`],[`is-flamer-ba`,`body`,`#1`]]},{$k:[`location`,`weapon`],$v:[[`ra`,`inf-auto-rifle`],[`la`,`inf-auto-rifle`]]},`Built on the AI Mk IIr chassis, the Mortar variant strips all direct-fire weaponry in favor of a pair of heavy mortars. This gives Kopis troopers an indirect fire capability rare among battle armor designs, allowing them to engage targets from behind cover or lob explosive rounds over obstacles. The reactive armor from the Mk IIr is retained, providing enhanced protection against the return fire that indirect engagements inevitably attract. Heavy battle claws serve as the suit's only close-quarters defense.

The Mortar configuration is best employed in a supporting role, delivering suppressive fire from protected positions while other units close with the enemy. The reactive armor's properties are specifically designed to counter fragmentation effects common to mortar and missile weapons, making this variant resilient against opposing indirect fire assets attempting counter-battery operations.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-heavy-mortar`,`body`,`#0`],[`is-heavy-mortar`,`body`,`#1`]]},{manufacturer:`Gortex Multi-E`,primaryFactory:`Kittery`},`The Krise is an Inner Sphere light exoskeleton designed for civilian emergency response operations. Produced by Gortex Multi-E on Kittery, the suit entered service in the late thirty-first century as a purpose-built rescue platform intended primarily for Federated Suns emergency response teams. Carrying no weapons, the Krise was equipped with a comprehensive array of search and rescue tools. Its straightforward controls allowed operators with minimal powered armor training to employ it effectively in the field.`,`The Krise weighs four hundred kilograms and carries a comprehensive set of emergency response tools. A cutting torch mounted on the right arm handles structural breaching and debris removal, while a salvage arm on the left limb manages heavier extraction tasks. Improved sensors detect life signs through dense material, and a searchlight assists operations in low-visibility conditions. Extended life support allows the operator to work in contaminated or oxygen-deprived environments for prolonged periods.

Jump jets provide a ninety-meter jump range alongside a thirty-kilometer-per-hour ground speed, giving the suit solid mobility for reaching disaster sites. Standard armor offers adequate protection for emergency work but cannot withstand sustained heat exposure the way Clan fire-resistant plating can. Brief contact with intense flames risks permanent damage to internal systems. The Krise carries no weapons, and its design emphasis on ease of use makes it accessible to emergency personnel who lack formal powered armor training.`,`The Clan Invasion triggered rapid advances in battle armor technology across the Inner Sphere, and these breakthroughs filtered down into civilian exoskeleton development. Gortex Multi-E, the Federated Suns' leading manufacturer of emergency services equipment, applied these lighter frame designs to create a purpose-built rescue exoskeleton. Christened the Krise, the suit was designed to assist emergency response teams in locating and extracting survivors from rubble, cave-ins, and natural disasters.

The Krise proved immediately popular with civilian agencies. Simple to operate and equipped with the tools needed for common rescue scenarios, the suit filled a gap that conventional equipment could not match. Emergency response teams across the Federated Suns adopted it quickly, and production runs sold out three years in advance. The suit's standard armor offered adequate protection for its intended role, though it lacked the fire resistance found on Clan-manufactured rescue exoskeletons and remained vulnerable to concentrated heat exposure that could permanently damage internal systems.`,[{tag:`quirk-easy-to-pilot`}],{la:{manipulator:`salvage-arm`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1},ra:{manipulator:`basic`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1}},{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-cutting-torch`,`ra`,`#0`],[`is-searchlight`,`la`,`#1`],[`is-extended-life-support`,`body`,`#2`],[`is-improved-sensors`,`body`,`#3`]]},{manufacturer:`Corean Enterprises|Etna Foundries|Irian BattleMechs Unlimited|Kali Yama Weapons Industries|Field Refit`,primaryFactory:`Stewart|Oriente|Irian|Kalidasa|Various`},`The Longinus was the Free Worlds League's answer to the Clan Elemental battle armor, developed jointly with the Word of Blake beginning in 3054. Classified as a medium battle armor, the suit came closer to matching its Clan-tech inspiration than any other Inner Sphere design of the era. The FWL's alliance with the Blakists provided access to technologies that rival programs in other Great Houses lacked. First fielded in 3057, the Longinus earned a reputation as both capable and expensive, with production costs well above comparable Inner Sphere designs.`,`The Magnetic variant replaces the Longinus's signature detachable SRM-2 launcher and modular weapon mount with magnetic clamps, a standard machine gun, and a heavy machine gun. The magnetic clamps allow troopers to ride on the exterior of conventional BattleMechs and vehicles, extending operational range without requiring OmniMech transport bays. With no launcher to jettison, the suit's jump jets are available immediately, giving troopers full ninety-meter leaps from the start of an engagement. The paired machine guns deliver considerable anti-infantry firepower at short range, though the loss of the SRM capability and modular flexibility limits effectiveness against armored targets. The left arm retains its battle claw for swarming attacks and the anti-personnel weapon mount. Advanced armor protection remains unchanged from the standard Longinus, maintaining the same Clan-equivalent composite that can absorb a large laser hit.`,`Development of the Longinus began in October 3054 as a joint venture between the Free Worlds League and the Word of Blake. The FWLM sought to replicate the Clan Elemental battle armor, and access to Blakist technologies gave the League an advantage over rival programs in the Federated Commonwealth and Draconis Combine. The advanced nature of the design and bureaucratic friction between the League and its allies slowed progress well beyond comparable efforts elsewhere.

The first prototypes reached testing in April 3056, and the results were catastrophic. Armor composites shattered during live-fire exercises, and power systems failed at random. Investigation traced many of these failures to a hidden ComStar sympathizer embedded in the Blakist development staff. Following the operative's removal, the team produced a corrected series of prototypes in early 3057. These early suits lacked the backpack missile launcher that made the Elemental so lethal, and the FWLM demanded one be incorporated. Other Inner Sphere teams had tried and failed to combine jump capability with a shoulder-mounted launcher, producing suits that were either too heavy on the ground or too ungainly in mid-jump. The Longinus team spent eight months on the problem before devising a detachable mounting that required jettisoning the launcher before the jump jets could engage.

By this point the project had far exceeded its budget, and the League Central Coordination Commission refused further funding. Production resumed only when Captain-General Thomas Marik personally authorized additional resources and IBMU agreed to absorb some costs in exchange for access to Blakist technology. The first production suits rolled off the Irian assembly line in December 3057. They arrived too late for Operation Guerrero but saw action in the Sirian Campaign and proved their worth during Operation Bulldog, where Longinus troopers held their own against Clan Elementals.

Corean Enterprises on Stewart and Kali-Yama Weapons Industries on Kalidasa soon picked up production, making the suit a common sight throughout the FWLM. Rear-echelon formations received small allotments for security, while elite frontline units fielded company or even battalion-strength deployments. The Longinus played a considerable role in the FWLM's success in the Isle of Skye. Thomas Marik initially refused to supply the suit to the Word of Blake for their planned assault on Terra, but his stance later softened, and Longinus suits began appearing in the Word of Blake Militia in significant numbers. During the Jihad, modified variants with magnetic clamps and experimental C³i electronics expanded the suit's operational profile, though the latter project was shelved after trials revealed excessive capability trade-offs.`,{la:{manipulator:`battle-claw`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1},ra:{manipulator:`none`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1}},{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-heavy-machine-gun`,`ra`,`#0`],[`is-machine-gun`,`la`,`#1`],[`is-magnetic-clamps`,`body`,`#2`]]},[{location:`la`,weapon:`inf-auto-rifle`}],{manufacturer:`Irian BattleMechs Unlimited|Corean Enterprises|Kali Yama Weapons Industries|Etna Foundries|Gibson Federated BattleMechs`,primaryFactory:`Irian|Stewart|Kalidasa|Oriente|Gibson`},`The Longinus carries a detachable one-shot SRM-2 on a dorsal rack that must be jettisoned before the suit's jump jets can engage, allowing leaps of up to ninety meters. The David configuration, fielded beginning in 3063, equips a "David" light Gauss rifle in the right-arm modular mount. This weapon gives the suit meaningful engagement range beyond what the flamer, laser, or machine gun options provide, allowing troopers to engage targets accurately at distances where return fire from infantry weapons is less effective. The left arm retains the standard battle claw for swarming attacks and an anti-personnel mount for close-range threats. Advanced armor composites give the Longinus protection comparable to Clan battle armor, absorbing a direct large laser hit. The combination of ranged firepower and heavy protection makes this configuration effective in open terrain where other loadouts would need to close the distance.`,{la:{manipulator:`none`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1},ra:{manipulator:`battle-claw`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1}},{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`shots`,`oneShot`,`detachable`],$v:[[`is-david-light-gauss-rifle`,`ra`,`#0`,null,null,null],[`is-srm-2`,`body`,`#1`,1,!0,!0]]},`The Longinus carries a detachable one-shot SRM-2 launcher on a specialized dorsal mounting, providing an opening salvo capability before close engagement. Internal safety mechanisms prevent the suit's jump jets from engaging until the launcher is expended and jettisoned, after which troopers can leap up to ninety meters. The Flamer configuration mounts a small flamer in the right-arm modular weapon system, well-suited for clearing infantry positions and flushing defenders from enclosed spaces. A battle claw on the left arm enables anti-BattleMech swarming attacks, supplemented by an anti-personnel weapon mount for engaging unarmored infantry. The suit's advanced armor composite provides protection roughly equivalent to Clan battle armor plating, with enough coverage to absorb a direct large laser hit. This protection comes at significant manufacturing cost, as the specialized composites require dedicated production processes.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`shots`,`oneShot`,`detachable`],$v:[[`is-flamer-ba`,`ra`,`#0`,null,null,null],[`is-srm-2`,`body`,`#1`,1,!0,!0]]},`The Longinus mounts a detachable one-shot SRM-2 on a dorsal rack, giving troopers an opening volley before the launcher is jettisoned to enable jump jet use. Once airborne, the suit can cover ninety meters per leap. The Laser configuration equips a small laser in the right-arm modular mount, offering reliable energy-based firepower with no ammunition dependency. This makes the configuration popular for extended operations where resupply is uncertain. The left arm carries a battle claw for anti-BattleMech attacks and an anti-personnel weapon mount for dealing with unarmored threats. Advanced armor composites protect the trooper against hits up to and including a large laser, closely matching Clan-grade protection levels. The weight and bulk of this armor limit the suit's ground speed, but the jump capability offsets this restriction in most tactical situations.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`shots`,`oneShot`,`detachable`],$v:[[`is-small-laser`,`ra`,`#0`,null,null,null],[`is-srm-2`,`body`,`#1`,1,!0,!0]]},`Troopers wearing the Longinus carry a one-shot SRM-2 on a detachable dorsal mounting. Internal safety interlocks prevent jump jet activation until the launcher is jettisoned, after which the suit can leap ninety meters. The Machine Gun configuration fits a light machine gun in the right-arm modular mount, providing sustained automatic fire effective against infantry formations and light vehicles. Ammunition supply limits endurance compared to energy-armed variants, but the volume of fire compensates in short engagements. A battle claw on the left arm supports anti-BattleMech swarming operations, while an anti-personnel weapon mount handles unarmored targets. The suit's advanced armor composite absorbs hits equivalent to a large laser strike, protection that closely mirrors Clan battle armor. Ground mobility is limited, but jump capability provides the tactical flexibility needed to close with heavier targets.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`shots`,`oneShot`,`detachable`],$v:[[`is-machine-gun`,`ra`,`#0`,null,null,null],[`is-srm-2`,`body`,`#1`,1,!0,!0]]},{manufacturer:`Marian Arms, Inc.|Pinard Protectorates Limited`,primaryFactory:`Horatius|Macleod's Land`},`The Marauder is a heavy battle armor suit developed by Marian Arms, Incorporated for the Marian Hegemony Armed Forces. Introduced in 3110 as the Hegemony's second indigenous battle armor design, the Marauder takes a markedly different approach from its predecessor. Where the Ravager emphasized survivability, the Marauder prioritizes offensive output and fire support capability. The suit takes its name from the iconic Marauder BattleMech, and its weapon layout pays deliberate homage to that venerable design. The Marauder has earned a solid reputation among Periphery military forces and the mercenary commands they employ.`,`Weighing 1,500 kilograms, the Marauder is built around delivering punishing firepower at the expense of both speed and protection. Each arm mounts a Support PPC paired with an Anti-Personnel Weapon Mount, while a Light Recoilless Rifle sits on the body. A Light TAG unit rounds out the loadout, allowing troopers to designate targets for Arrow IV missiles and semi-guided long-range munitions.

Ground speed is limited to just 11 km/h, making the Marauder one of the slower suits in active service. Magnetic clamps partially offset this limitation by allowing troopers to ride friendly vehicles or BattleMechs into the fight. Armor protection sits at roughly half that of the Ravager, a conscious trade-off that has drawn criticism from field commanders. The open chassis design does make the suit easy to service, earning it high marks from maintenance crews who appreciate the accessibility of internal systems.`,`The Marauder grew out of the Marian Hegemony's expanding battle armor program, which began with Dr. Dafyyd Rou's development of the Ravager on Horatius in the mid-3080s. Building on lessons learned from their first suit, Marian Arms engineers pursued a lighter design that prioritized offensive capability over raw durability. The result entered service in 3110, drawing its name and weapon configuration from the iconic Marauder BattleMech.

The suit saw its first major combat deployment during the Landfall rebellion of 3117, when Free Leaguer dissidents seized large portions of the planet. II Legio deployed Marauder squads during the bloody street fighting in New Erewhon, where the suits proved decisive against rebel BattleMechs. The capture of the rebel leader during that engagement was broadcast across the Hegemony, bringing the Marauder to widespread public attention.

Marian Arms licensed the design to Pinard Protectorates Limited in the Taurian Concordat, and both nations sell the suit freely to mercenaries in their employ.`,{$k:[`tag`,`equipmentUuid`],$v:[[`quirk-easy-to-maintain`,null],[`quirk-accurate-weapon`,`#0`]]},{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-support-ppc`,`ra`,`#1`],[`is-support-ppc`,`la`,`#2`],[`is-light-recoilless-rifle`,`body`,`#0`],[`is-light-tag`,`body`,`#3`],[`is-magnetic-clamps`,`body`,`#4`]]},{manufacturer:`Gibson Federated BattleMechs`,primaryFactory:`Gibson`},`The Demon series of battle armor emerged from Gibson Federated BattleMechs during the Jihad as the companion line to the Word of Blake's Celestial OmniMechs. Conceived by Precentor Vapula, the Manei Domini's engineering chief, the six Demon designs draw their names from the malevolent figures of various world mythologies. Each suit was crafted to fill a specific combat role while projecting an element of psychological terror against the Word's enemies. Fielded almost exclusively by the cybernetically enhanced Manei Domini elite, the Demons share the same unsettling visual aesthetic as their larger Celestial counterparts and were rarely encountered outside the Shadow Divisions.`,`The capture team configuration makes aggressive use of detachable weapon technology, mounting a battle armor taser in the right arm alongside a two-tube LRM launcher in a detachable pack. A heavy machine gun resides in the right modular mount for conventional engagements. The detachable pack's bulk restricts mobility until jettisoned. The taser can disable enemy vehicles and force BattleMech shutdowns through electromagnetic overload, though the risk of feedback injury to the operator is considerable. The 850 kilograms of mimetic armor provides protection and concealment during the approach phase. This is one of the most specialized Nephilim configurations, designed to seize enemy equipment and personnel intact for interrogation or reverse engineering. A fully staffed squad can potentially disable multiple enemy units in rapid succession.`,`Alongside its counterpart the Shedu, the Nephilim rounded out the Demon series with its entry into the assault class. Debuting last in the entire line, the Nephilim's first recorded appearance came during fighting on Gibson in mid-3071. Even at that early stage, the suit's powerful build, thick sensor-defeating armor, and range of equipment configurations quickly identified it as perhaps the pinnacle of Vapula's creations.

The Nephilim was the Demon that came closest to fulfilling Vapula's original vision for the entire program. Stealthy, mobile, and well armored, its generous use of modular and detachable weapons emphasized a flexibility that the other Demons only dabbled in. Where the Tengu had been the experimental proof of concept for Celestial-like adaptability in battle armor, the Nephilim was the refined production model. Six basic configurations were identified over the course of the Jihad, and the suit's modular nature spawned an even wider variety of one-off field variants observed in engagements across the Inner Sphere. Opposing commanders learned to tell their troops only that they could expect missile or rocket fire from suits shrouded in heavy sensor-defeating armor, because predicting the specific loadout of any given Nephilim formation was essentially impossible.

Even the suit's name reinforced the connection to the Celestials. Taken from Christian legends of half-angels who lived among humanity, the designation hinted at the Nephilim's closer relation to the Celestial OmniMechs than any other Demon design, and likely at the engineering goals Vapula had aimed for when developing the entire series. If the Celestials were the angels of the Word's arsenal, the Nephilim was the bridge between those divine machines and the infernal battlesuits that fought at their feet.

Despite its capabilities, the Nephilim had one of the lowest distribution rates in the entire Demon series. As the most recently introduced design, production time had been limited, and the suit never appeared in the ranks of the regular Word of Blake Militia or Protectorate Militia. Even the Shadow Divisions lacked widespread access. Allied intelligence placed the largest confirmed concentrations in the Forty-fourth, Forty-seventh, Forty-ninth, and Fifty-second Shadow Divisions only. Commanders who faced the Nephilim in battle quickly discovered that the suits were too formidable for most other battle armor to contend with. Medium-weight combat vehicles and BattleMechs were considered the most effective means of countering these assault-class Demons.`,{la:{manipulator:`heavy-battle-claw`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1},ra:{manipulator:`none`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1}},{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`shots`,`oneShot`,`dwp`],$v:[[`is-heavy-machine-gun`,`ra`,`#0`,null,null,null],[`is-lrm-2`,`body`,`#1`,1,!0,null],[`is-taser`,`body`,`#2`,null,null,!0]]},`The "Gauss Nephilim" replaces the standard configuration's missile-and-plasma combination with three ballistic weapons for precise, sustained anti-armor fire. A "David" light Gauss rifle in the right arm provides the primary punch, while two Magshot Gauss rifles over the shoulders deliver supplementary kinetic damage. This loadout sacrifices the burst damage of the standard model's SRM volleys for more consistent accuracy and armor penetration across multiple engagements. The 850 kilograms of mimetic armor and enhanced ground speed are unchanged. The all-Gauss weapons suite eliminates concerns about heat buildup, allowing the trooper to maintain a steady rate of fire. The precision of this configuration made it particularly valued by the Manei Domini elite, whose cybernetic enhancements could fully exploit the weapons' accuracy potential.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-david-light-gauss-rifle`,`ra`,`#0`],[`is-magshot-gauss-rifle`,`body`,`#1`],[`is-magshot-gauss-rifle`,`body`,`#2`]]},`The Narc variant carries a two-shot light Narc launcher in a detachable mount and a support PPC in a detachable weapon pack, supplemented by a twin-tube rocket launcher. The detachable systems impose mobility penalties until dropped. The Narc beacon allows the trooper to tag enemy units for guided munitions from allied forces, combining the spotter role with direct firepower from the support PPC. A Magshot Gauss rifle in a modular turret mount adds precision ballistic capability. The 850 kilograms of mimetic armor is retained. This configuration bridges the gap between the dedicated C3-equipped spotting role and direct combat, providing the squad with both target designation capability and enough personal firepower to defend itself if the engagement turns unfavorable.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`dwp`,`shots`],$v:[[`is-support-ppc`,`body`,`#0`,!0,null],[`is-rocket-launcher-2`,`body`,`#1`,null,null],[`is-compact-narc`,`body`,`#2`,null,2]]},`Dubbed the "Seeker," this configuration transforms the Nephilim into a dedicated hunter-killer platform. A machine gun in the right modular mount provides anti-infantry capability, while an SRM-3 launcher with two reloads delivers sustained anti-armor fire over multiple engagements. Improved sensors and a searchlight mounted in a drum over the left shoulder enhance target detection and tracking in all conditions. The 850 kilograms of mimetic armor and enhanced ground speed are retained. The combination of enhanced sensors and sensor-defeating armor creates a trooper who can detect enemies while remaining difficult to detect in return. The Seeker is optimized for tracking and eliminating specific high-value targets rather than general combat, making it a preferred configuration for the Domini's more surgically minded operatives.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`shots`],$v:[[`is-machine-gun`,`ra`,`#0`,null],[`is-srm-3`,`body`,`#1`,3],[`is-improved-sensors`,`body`,`#2`,null],[`is-searchlight`,`body`,`#3`,null]]},`The support variant forgoes modular mounts entirely, instead carrying a fixed small laser in the right arm and two detachable four-tube rocket launchers. This gives the Nephilim a powerful opening salvo followed by indefinite energy-based firepower once the rockets are expended. The fixed mounting reduces configuration flexibility but simplifies the suit's internal systems. The 850 kilograms of mimetic armor and enhanced ground speed are unchanged. Once the rocket launchers are jettisoned, the suit becomes lighter and faster, able to press the engagement with its small laser at closer range. The combination of expendable ordnance and a reliable energy weapon makes this a practical combat loadout that requires less logistical support than the SRM-dependent Seeker variant.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-small-laser`,`ra`,`#0`],[`is-rocket-launcher-4`,`body`,`#1`],[`is-rocket-launcher-4`,`body`,`#2`]]},{manufacturer:`Krupp Armament Works|Comguard Tukayyid Battle Armor Production Facility|Free Worlds Defence Industries|Arc-Royal MechWorks|Niops Project Workshop III`,primaryFactory:`Terra|Tukayyid|Gibson|Arc-Royal|Niops V`},`The Nighthawk Mk. XXI PA(L) is the progenitor of modern battle armor, developed by the Terran Hegemony from commercial exoskeleton technology through one of the most challenging military engineering programs of the Star League era. Commissioned in 2720, the Nighthawk was designed as a light power armor suit for reconnaissance and covert operations. The suit entered service exclusively with the SLDF Special Forces Command, where it equipped elite special operations teams whose very existence remained classified. Classified as Power Armor (Light), the Nighthawk provided its operators with capabilities far beyond conventional infantry while maintaining the stealth profile necessary for behind-the-lines work, establishing the conceptual foundation for every battle armor design that followed.`,`The Mk. XXI weighs four hundred kilograms and mounts Standard Stealth armor incorporating both infrared suppression and electronic countermeasures. An integral ECM suite further enhances the trooper's ability to avoid detection, making the Nighthawk exceptionally difficult to locate through conventional sensor sweeps. Jump jets allow leaps of up to ninety meters, providing excellent mobility across broken terrain and urban environments. The suit carries no integral weapons, relying instead on a pair of armored gloves that allow troopers to wield standard infantry arms, typically the Mauser 960 assault system, without loss of dexterity. Extended life support enables prolonged independent operations behind enemy lines.

The Nighthawk's protection is adequate against small arms fire but offers little resistance to crew-served weapons or BattleMech-scale armaments, limiting the suit's effectiveness to roles where stealth and evasion take priority over direct engagement.`,`The Nighthawk program grew from First Star League efforts to militarize commercial exoskeletons that had been in civilian use since the early space age. First Lord Jonathan Cameron's Terran Hegemony funded an ambitious development effort, though the technology proved stubbornly difficult to adapt for combat. Fifteen years of engineering work and twenty failed prototypes preceded the breakthrough that produced the Mk. XXI in 2718. After two years of field testing, the suit entered full production at Krupp Armament Works on Terra and was issued to the Special Forces Command in 2720.

The SLDF deployed Nighthawks exclusively through its Special Armed Services, the covert operators known as the Blackhearts. The deployment was never publicly acknowledged, and the suit's existence remained classified throughout its Star League service life. The men and women of the SAS operated with such skill that the other Star League Member States heard only vague rumors of some advanced sneak suit. Rumors persist that the Thirteenth Royal Infantry Division, stationed deep in the Draconis Combine, was among the first formations to field-test the Nighthawk, conducting secret exercises against another Royal infantry division somewhere in the Draconis Rift. Plans for a combat-oriented variant, the Mk. XXII, were drawn up for distribution to SLDF Royal Divisions. A working prototype reached completion in 2749, but the Amaris Coup and the collapse of the Star League prevented that version from entering standard production.

When the Succession Wars erupted, both variants were thought lost. The Nighthawk research, however, accompanied the SLDF on the Exodus, becoming the seed from which the Clans would eventually develop true battle armor. On Terra, Jerome Blake's newly formed ComStar uncovered a small number of surviving suits and used them to equip commando units following the creation of the Com Guards. ComStar lacked the manufacturing capability to produce replacements, and attempts to reverse-engineer the design yielded only the inferior Tornado PA(L).

The Gray Death Legion recovered several Nighthawk suits on Karbala during the later Succession Wars. Most were turned over to the New Avalon Institute of Science for study, providing Davion engineers with critical data on power armor technology. New production finally resumed in 3065 when the Word of Blake, working in conjunction with the Free Worlds League, overcame the technical challenges and restarted manufacturing on Terra and Gibson. ComStar responded by establishing a production facility on Tukayyid, sharing output with the Second Star League and deploying Mk. XXIs to several Fury Teams. That factory operated for barely two years before the Word of Blake destroyed it in 3067.

During the Jihad, the Word of Blake supplied Nighthawk suits to Protectorate Militia infantry units, using the light power armor to bolster garrison forces while reserving heavier battle armor designs for frontline formations. ComStar, crippled by the loss of Tukayyid and the casualties suffered during Case White, took years to restart production at Arc-Royal MechWorks. Even after the facility came online, output remained limited, with priority going to personnel engaged in covert operations.

The Niops Association provided an unexpected additional source beginning around 3071, when engineers discovered Star League-era production records in their archives. Niops Project Workshop III began manufacturing both the Mk. XXI and the Mk. XXII, primarily to defend against belligerent Periphery neighbors. The decision proved wise when the Marian Hegemony's VI Legio Ripariensis raided Niops V that same year. The legionnaires were completely unprepared for power-armored infantry, and the Nighthawk-equipped defenders routed the invaders. Small numbers of captured Nighthawks from Word of Blake stockpiles have since appeared on the open arms market, finding buyers among mercenary commands, private security firms, and militia forces willing to acquire any available battle armor technology.`,{la:{manipulator:`armored-glove`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1},ra:{manipulator:`armored-glove`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1}},{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-ecm-suite`,`body`,`#0`],[`is-extended-life-support`,`body`,`#1`]]},{manufacturer:`Krupp Armament Works`,primaryFactory:`Terra`},`The Mk. XXX is a mobility-focused evolution of the Nighthawk platform. While retaining the Standard Stealth armor and ninety-meter jump capability common to the Nighthawk family, the Mk. XXX features a significantly improved ground speed of thirty kilometers per hour, roughly three times the pace of the Mk. XXI. This enhanced overland mobility makes the suit more effective for extended patrol routes and rapid ground-level repositioning between jump points, allowing troopers to cover greater distances without consuming jump jet fuel reserves.

Like other Nighthawk variants, the suit carries no integral weapons, depending on armored gloves and standard infantry arms for any combat requirement. The Mk. XXX remains just as vulnerable to heavy weapons as its siblings, filling a useful niche for missions where sustained ground movement takes priority over the stop-and-leap pattern typical of jump-equipped power armor.`,{manufacturer:`Tvastar Enterprises`,primaryFactory:`Regulus`},`Tvastar Enterprises' first entry into the battle armor market, the Ogre was designed to supplement slower vehicle platoons and fill gaps in Regulan planetary militias. Introduced in 3091, this heavy Inner Sphere suit was built on Regulus during a period of increasing reliance on non-BattleMech forces across the former Free Worlds League. The Ogre's unusually wide frame and paired melee weapons give it an intimidating battlefield presence that belies its intended support role. Though ill-suited to anti-infantry work, the suit earned a fearsome, if exaggerated, combat reputation through its effectiveness against armored vehicles.`,`The Interdictor configuration replaces a portion of the standard Ogre's SRM ammunition with an ECM Suite and upgrades the suit's protection to Mimetic Armor. This electronic warfare package disrupts enemy targeting systems and communications, while the adaptive camouflage allows troopers to close on targets with reduced detection. The combination enables the Interdictor to maneuver into more favorable positions than its standard counterpart before engaging.

The trade-off for this stealth capability is a significantly reduced missile loadout, limiting the Interdictor's ability to sustain prolonged firefights. The suit retains its paired Heavy Battle Claws and ground-level mobility profile, making it best employed in ambush or screening roles where its electronic warfare suite can disrupt enemy coordination before close-range engagement.`,`Tvastar Enterprises developed the Ogre in the years following the Jihad, capitalizing on a growing market for non-BattleMech combat units across the former Free Worlds League. The suit performed far better than anticipated, and Tvastar soon secured export contracts with the Duchy of Andurien and the Lyran Commonwealth.

The Ogre's tank-shredding reputation traces to a 3095 engagement on the Oriente Protectorate world of Emris IV. The Tenth Regulan Hussars deployed their Ogre troopers during an exploratory raid that escalated into contact with Steel Guard forces. Three Ogre squads ambushed by Vedette tanks in a narrow defile used their suits' heavy claws to disable and tear open the immobilized vehicles. BattleROM footage of the aftermath gave birth to the myth that Ogres could literally rip tanks in half, and the ritual destruction of disabled vehicles became a tradition among Ogre operators.

A small number of Ogres eventually reached the Rasalhague Dominion through merchant channels, finding an unexpected second market among Clan warriors who valued the suit's physical presence on the battlefield.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`shots`],$v:[[`is-srm-2`,`body`,`#0`,8],[`is-ecm-suite`,`body`,`#1`,null]]},`The Ogre carries paired Heavy Battle Claws as its primary melee weapons, capable of inflicting serious damage to vehicle armor at close range. An SRM-2 launcher with thirteen volleys of ammunition provides the suit's ranged striking power, giving operators enough firepower for sustained engagements. Nearly a full ton of standard armor provides considerable protection, allowing the Ogre to absorb fire from all but the heaviest vehicle-mounted weapons.

At 1,500 kilograms, the Ogre is classified as a heavy battle armor suit. Its ground speed of 22 kilometers per hour and complete lack of jump capability limit tactical mobility, making the suit dependent on transport or supporting units to close with targets. Despite its imposing size, the suit lacks anti-personnel weapons, leaving it poorly matched against conventional infantry.`,[{tag:`is-srm-2`,location:`body`,uuid:`#0`,shots:13}],{manufacturer:`Luthien Armor Works|Luthien Armor Works`,primaryFactory:`Luthien|Savinsville`},`Produced by Luthien Armor Works for the Draconis Combine Mustered Soldiery, the Oni is a medium battlesuit that entered service in 3134 as a replacement for the aging Raiden. The one-ton humanoid design employs mixed Inner Sphere and Clan technology, incorporating advanced protective plating to improve operator survivability. Intended as a flexible line unit, the Oni fills multiple battlefield roles through interchangeable weapon loadouts rather than any single specialization. The suit's name translates from Japanese as "ogre," a designation its operators have found grimly appropriate given the design's imposing but graceless silhouette.`,`The Bearhunter configuration mounts a Bearhunter Superheavy Autocannon on the right-arm modular weapon mount, fed by twenty rounds of ammunition. This loadout punishes other battle armor and conventional infantry at short range, delivering fire that can shred lighter suits quickly. A left-arm Heavy Vibro Claw handles close-quarters fighting, while a basic manipulator on the weapon arm allows interaction with equipment and OmniMech handholds for mechanized transport.

Three hundred and fifty kilograms of Clan-specification fire-resistant armor absorbs punishment from both conventional weapons and incendiary attacks. An ECM suite disrupts enemy targeting, and extended life support permits prolonged deployment in hostile environments. Ground speed of thirty-two kilometers per hour is adequate but unremarkable, and the Oni lacks any jump capability, limiting tactical mobility in rough terrain.`,`The DCMS commissioned Luthien Armor Works to develop a Raiden replacement during the early 3130s. Engineers drew on data from evaluations of the Clan Nova Cat Thunderbird suit, adapting several design principles for Inner Sphere production. The Oni entered service in 3134, with manufacturing lines on both Luthien and Savinsville.

Reception within the DCMS was poor. The Combine's warrior culture prizes honor and prestige, and the Oni's unremarkable capabilities and ungainly appearance earned it few admirers. The Procurement Department reinforced the suit's low standing by assigning it to garrison commands and units out of favor. Supply difficulties compounded these problems, as the Department shipped weapon loadouts based on its own assessments rather than unit requests. Quartermasters across the New Samarkand and Dieron Military Districts developed an informal gray market for trading modular weapon packs among themselves.

The suit's first recorded action came during the Combine's push to reclaim former Dieron Military District holdings. Positions fielding the Oni showed measurably higher survivability rates among defending personnel, and within a year the suit had been distributed to every planetary garrison across both districts. Heavy protective plating proved its strongest selling point, keeping troopers alive long enough to shift opinions among pragmatic field commanders.`,{$k:[`tag`],$v:[[`quirk-fast-reload`],[`quirk-non-standard-parts`]]},{la:{manipulator:`heavy-battle-claw-vibro`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1},ra:{manipulator:`basic`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1}},{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`clan-bearhunter-superheavy-ac`,`ra`,`#0`],[`is-ecm-suite`,`body`,`#1`],[`is-extended-life-support`,`body`,`#2`]]},`The medium recoilless rifle configuration equips the Oni's modular weapon mount with a Medium Recoilless Rifle and twenty rounds of ammunition, providing a balanced engagement profile against both armored and soft targets. The rifle offers greater effective range than the autocannon variant while retaining reasonable stopping power. A Heavy Vibro Claw on the left arm gives troopers a melee option, and the right arm's basic manipulator supports mechanized operations aboard OmniMechs.

The suit's three hundred and fifty kilograms of Clan-produced fire-resistant armor is its strongest defensive feature, providing protection well above what most Inner Sphere medium suits carry. An integrated ECM suite and extended life support round out the electronics package. At thirty-two kilometers per hour with no jump jets, the Oni relies on ground mobility alone, making terrain selection critical for effective employment.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-ecm-suite`,`body`,`#0`],[`is-extended-life-support`,`body`,`#1`],[`is-medium-recoilless-rifle`,`ra`,`#2`]]},`The Narc configuration fits a Compact Narc Launcher with four missile packs onto the modular weapon mount, shifting the Oni's role from direct combatant to target designator. Narc beacons attached to enemy units guide friendly missile fire with improved accuracy, making this variant valuable in combined-arms operations despite its limited ammunition count. The left-arm Heavy Vibro Claw provides a backup melee capability, while the right-arm basic manipulator supports mechanized transport.

Three hundred and fifty kilograms of Clan fire-resistant armor keeps troopers protected during the dangerous approach runs required to place beacons at effective range. An ECM suite and extended life support complete the electronics fit. Ground speed of thirty-two kilometers per hour and the absence of jump jets make closing with targets a challenge, placing a premium on coordination with faster friendly units.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`shots`],$v:[[`is-extended-life-support`,`body`,`#0`,null],[`is-ecm-suite`,`body`,`#1`,null],[`is-compact-narc`,`ra`,`#2`,4]]},`The particle projector configuration arms the Oni with a Support PPC and fourteen power packs through its modular weapon mount, giving troopers an energy weapon capable of engaging targets at longer ranges than the ballistic alternatives. The Support PPC hits hard for a battle armor weapon, though its limited ammunition supply demands careful fire discipline. The left-arm Heavy Vibro Claw remains effective in close combat, and a right-arm basic manipulator allows mechanized transport on OmniMechs.

Clan-specification fire-resistant armor at three hundred and fifty kilograms provides the Oni's primary survivability advantage, shrugging off incendiary weapons and absorbing considerable punishment. The onboard ECM suite helps mask the trooper's electronic signature, while extended life support sustains operations in vacuum or contaminated atmospheres. Without jump jets, the suit's thirty-two kilometer per hour ground speed is its only mobility option.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-ecm-suite`,`body`,`#0`],[`is-extended-life-support`,`body`,`#1`],[`is-support-ppc`,`ra`,`#2`]]},`The Phalanx is a heavy battle armor suit developed by Irian Technologies for the Free Worlds League Military. Entering production in 3065, the design filled a gap in the FWLM’s battle armor roster for a heavier platform capable of delivering sustained ranged firepower. Weighing 1,500 kilograms, the suit trades jump capability for ground speed and mounting flexibility, allowing troopers to ride OmniMechs into combat while retaining the ability to handle infantry-scale weapons. Advanced stealth protection makes the suit considerably harder to detect and engage than most contemporary heavy designs, though this comes at the cost of reduced overall durability.`,`The Free Worlds League Military began development of a heavy battle armor suit in 3059, seeking a domestic counter to designs like the Kanazuchi and Gnome. Irian Technologies led the effort, but persistent technical failures stalled the program for over two years. Processor malfunctions and myomer musculature breakdowns proved particularly resistant to resolution. The FWLM eventually permitted Word of Blake technical teams to join the project, and the Blakist scientists resolved the remaining issues with striking efficiency.

Production commenced in 3065 at Irian Technologies’ facilities on Irian, with the Word of Blake receiving purchasing rights as payment for their assistance. Free Worlds Defense Industries on Gibson began manufacturing shortly afterward, producing both the original configuration and a dedicated Blakist variant. During the Jihad, the Word of Blake expanded its use of the suit as a heavy fire support complement to lighter Purifier and Nighthawk designs. Alys Rousset-Marik’s resistance movement also acquired significant numbers of the original configuration, using them to provide fire support against protectorate militias.

Post-Jihad production continued under new political oversight. A second Irian BattleMechs Unlimited facility on Washburn came online around 3078, and the design remained in active service through the Dark Age. A modernized configuration appeared around 3132, proving popular with garrison and patrol commands across multiple successor states.`,{la:{manipulator:`battle-claw`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1},ra:{manipulator:`armored-glove`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1}},[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`shots`],{manufacturer:`Free Worlds Defence Industries|Irian BattleMechs Unlimited`,primaryFactory:`Gibson|Washburn`},`The Phalanx C configuration shifts the suit into an indirect fire support role, mounting a heavy mortar alongside a copy of the Magshot gauss rifle. The mortar allows troopers to deliver fire from behind cover and over obstacles, a capability few battle armor designs can match. The Magshot provides direct-fire backup with respectable accuracy at medium range.

This loadout transforms the suit from a frontline skirmisher into a fire support platform, though the mortar’s minimum range and relatively low rate of fire demand careful positioning. Mobility and defensive characteristics remain unchanged from the base chassis: 22 km/h ground movement, improved stealth armor for reduced sensor visibility, and the standard battle claw and armored glove combination for OmniMech transport and infantry weapon handling. The configuration lacks anti-BattleMech capability, relying instead on standoff distance and its stealth profile for survivability.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-heavy-mortar`,`body`,`#0`],[`is-magshot-gauss-rifle`,`ra`,`#1`]]},{manufacturer:`Irian BattleMechs Unlimited`,primaryFactory:`Irian`},`The modernized Phalanx D strips the suit’s missile and gauss weaponry in favor of a single detachable medium variable speed pulse laser. This energy weapon provides flexible engagement profiles, with adjustable pulse rates allowing troopers to balance accuracy against raw damage output depending on combat conditions. The simplified armament eliminates ammunition dependency, reducing logistical demands compared to earlier configurations.

A single battle claw replaces the original’s mixed manipulator arrangement, maintaining OmniMech transport capability. The base chassis characteristics carry forward: 22 km/h ground speed, improved stealth armor, and no jump capability. The configuration sacrifices the ranged versatility of earlier variants for a self-contained energy platform that can operate for extended periods without resupply, making it well suited for garrison and patrol duties where ammunition supply lines may be unreliable.`,[{tag:`is-medium-variable-speed-pulse-laser`,location:`body`,uuid:`#0`,dwp:!0}],{manufacturer:`Waytani, Inc.|Waytani, Inc.`,primaryFactory:`Marik|Pollux`},`The P-5000 PowerLoader is an industrial exoskeleton produced by Waytani, Inc., a Free Worlds League corporation formed specifically to manufacture and market the design. Introduced in 3054, the 400-kilogram humanoid suit was built for cargo handling in commercial docking facilities and industrial warehouses. The PowerLoader distinguished itself from competing loader exoskeletons by offering both operator protection and greater mobility, appealing to buyers who valued worker safety alongside raw lifting capacity. The design earned a strong commercial reputation as one of the Inner Sphere's premier cargo loading platforms.`,`The P-5000 trades some raw lifting capacity for improved mobility and operator safety. Its paired cargo lifter manipulators handle loads of up to two metric tons, and the suit moves at roughly double the walking speed of competing loader designs. Fifty kilograms of standard armor protect the operator from falling cargo, shifting loads, and other common workplace hazards that leave unarmored exoskeleton operators vulnerable. A cutting torch provides basic utility for opening containers and cutting through packing materials.

At 400 kilograms, the PowerLoader occupies the same weight class as its primary rival but distributes that mass differently, prioritizing the operator's speed and survivability over maximum payload. The lack of jump capability is typical for loader-class exoskeletons, where stability under load matters more than vertical mobility. The result is a loader well suited to fast-paced commercial docking facilities where both efficiency and safety standards are high.`,`Waytani, Inc. was established in the Free Worlds League with one purpose: producing and selling the P-5000 PowerLoader. The company found immediate success in the League and Capellan Confederation, where the design's combination of mobility and operator protection appealed to industrial buyers willing to trade some lifting capacity for a more capable platform. Waytani's aggressive holovid advertising campaign during the mid-3050s proved remarkably effective, generating enough revenue to fund construction of a second manufacturing facility on Pollux in 3060.

The PowerLoader and General Motors' HeavyHauler became the two dominant loader exoskeletons on the commercial market. Both designs featured distinctive lifter arm configurations that drew numerous imitators, though court rulings across the Inner Sphere consistently upheld the original manufacturers' intellectual property. Following the Jihad, Waytani leveraged its exoskeleton expertise to develop the Exo HaulerMech, an ultralight IndustrialMech intended for the same heavy-lifting role in more demanding environments. The PowerLoader itself remained a fixture at commercial loading facilities across the Inner Sphere.`,{la:{manipulator:`cargo-lifter`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:4},ra:{manipulator:`cargo-lifter`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:4}},[{tag:`is-cutting-torch`,location:`body`,uuid:`#0`}],{manufacturer:`Krupp Armament Works|HDF Manufacturing`,primaryFactory:`Terra|Falsterbo`},`The Purifier Adaptive is a medium battle armor design developed by the Word of Blake, built around a mimetic armor system capable of shifting color to match its surroundings. First fielded in 3062 from the Krupp Armament Works on Terra, the suit was among the first combat systems to employ adaptive camouflage technology, allowing troopers to become nearly invisible when stationary. Though lightly protected compared to other medium suits, the Purifier compensates with stealth and jump capability, filling a specialized reconnaissance and infiltration role. Its unique capabilities have made the Purifier one of the most distinctive battle armor suits in service across the Inner Sphere.`,`The Laser configuration equips the Purifier Adaptive's modular weapon port with an extended-range small laser, the suit's only vehicle-scale armament option. This energy weapon requires no ammunition, allowing troopers to sustain fire through extended engagements without logistical concerns. Combined with the mimetic armor, which makes stationary troopers exceptionally difficult to target, the Laser configuration excels at patient ambush tactics where concealment outweighs raw firepower. The suit's battle claw enables anti-BattleMech attacks during swarming operations, and jump jets provide ninety-meter leaps for rapid repositioning. Protection is relatively light for a medium suit, trading armor mass for the camouflage system that defines the design. The Laser loadout is considered the most self-sufficient configuration, requiring no resupply in the field.`,`The origins of the Purifier Adaptive trace to ComStar's Sandhurst Royal Military College on Terra, where scientists began developing mimetic color-shifting technology for infantry use in 3058. When elements of the Word of Blake overran the college, the Sandhurst Training Regiment fought to defend it but could not prevent the research data from falling into enemy hands. ComStar believed the project had been destroyed along with the facility.

Word of Blake engineers had gained considerable experience in battle armor development through their joint program with the Free Worlds League Military to produce the Achileus light suit. That expertise proved critical in adapting ComStar's mimetic research into a functional battle armor camouflage system. Development proceeded at the Krupp Armament Works in Germany, where Blakist technicians refined the technology through extensive testing before settling on a final production design. A prototype was revealed in August 3061, and the Purifier entered full production the following year.

Production at Krupp proceeded at an advanced pace, with the Word of Blake placing large quantities of the suit into every Division. The Purifier quickly earned a feared reputation during the Jihad, where its camouflage capabilities made it deadly in ambush operations and urban fighting. Troopers learned to exploit the suit's stealth by holding position until targets entered effective range, then striking before the enemy could react. Field commanders learned to manage the suit's operational limitations through disciplined tactical doctrine, maximizing the camouflage advantage under favorable conditions.

Following the liberation of Terra and the dissolution of the Word of Blake, significant numbers of Purifier suits fell into Coalition hands. The Republic of the Sphere proved the most enthusiastic adopter, capturing enough suits to equip multiple formations within the Republic Armed Forces. Republic commanders reorganized their Purifier troopers into four-member squads, mirroring standard Inner Sphere infantry doctrine rather than the Blakist six-trooper formation structure. Aside from this organizational change, the Republic operated the suit identically to the original Word of Blake configuration.

Production eventually expanded beyond Terra. HDF Manufacturing on Falsterbo began producing the Purifier Adaptive during the later Republic era, and additional manufacturers including Brevard Industries and Paragon Defense also took up production to meet growing demand. By the ilClan era, the Purifier Adaptive had spread well beyond its Blakist origins, finding service with multiple factions who valued its unique stealth capabilities.`,[{tag:`is-er-small-laser`,location:`la`,uuid:`#0`}],`The Narc configuration mounts a compact Narc missile beacon launcher in the Purifier Adaptive's modular weapon port, transforming the suit into a dedicated missile spotter. The launcher carries a single reload, and its bulk prevents the trooper from executing anti-BattleMech attacks or using jump jets until the ammunition is expended and the launcher jettisoned. This significant limitation restricts mobility during the approach phase of any engagement. Once a Narc beacon is attached to a target, allied missile platforms gain a substantial accuracy bonus against the marked unit. The mimetic armor helps Narc-equipped troopers close to effective range undetected, compensating somewhat for the temporary loss of jump capability. After the launcher is jettisoned, the trooper regains full mobility but is left with only the suit's battle claw for offensive action.`,[{tag:`is-compact-narc`,location:`la`,uuid:`#0`,shots:2}],`The PPC configuration is the newest addition to the Purifier Adaptive's weapon options, mounting an infantry-scale support particle projector cannon in the modular weapon port. Though classified as an infantry weapon rather than a vehicle-scale system, the PPC provides effective standoff capability that pairs well with the suit's mimetic armor. Troopers can remain motionless and nearly invisible at range while delivering accurate fire, making this the preferred loadout for sniping operations. The suit retains its battle claw for close-quarters anti-BattleMech attacks and jump jets for ninety-meter repositioning leaps. As with all Purifier configurations, armor protection is light for a medium suit, but the PPC's engagement range allows troopers to keep hostile units at a distance where the thin armor matters less.`,[{tag:`is-support-ppc`,location:`la`,uuid:`#0`}],`The TAG configuration equips the Purifier Adaptive's modular weapon port with a light target acquisition gear system, enabling troopers to designate targets for artillery strikes and semi-guided missile fire from allied units. This configuration carries no direct-fire weapons beyond the suit's battle claw, relying entirely on supporting forces for offensive action. The mimetic armor proves especially valuable in this role, as TAG operators must maintain line of sight to their targets while remaining undetected. Jump jets provide ninety-meter leaps for reaching elevated observation positions, and the trooper's low signature allows extended spotting without drawing counterfire. The TAG loadout demands coordination with artillery and missile assets, making it less useful for independent operations but devastating when integrated into a combined-arms force.`,[{tag:`is-light-tag`,location:`la`,uuid:`#0`}],{manufacturer:`StarCorp Industries`,primaryFactory:`Terra`},`The Quirinus is a medium-weight Inner Sphere battle armor produced by StarCorp Industries on Terra. Entering service in 3093, the suit was designed as a general-purpose trooper platform for the open market rather than exclusive military procurement. Its medium frame balances protection and mobility, offering jump-capable movement and armor well suited to its weight class. A modular weapon mount allows field commanders to tailor the suit's primary armament to mission requirements, giving the Quirinus a generalist flexibility valued by military and mercenary organizations alike.`,`The David Light Gauss Rifle configuration gives the Quirinus a long-range precision strike capability uncommon among medium battle armor. The rifle's ballistic reach allows troopers to engage targets at distances where most battle armor weapons fall short, while the suit's integral Light TAG designator enables effective coordination with supporting artillery and missile units. Four hundred eighty kilograms of reactive armor provide strong protection against explosive ordnance, though the suit remains more vulnerable to direct energy weapon fire. A vibro-enhanced battle claw on the right arm allows anti-BattleMech swarming attacks, and jump jets provide ninety meters of leaping mobility to offset the suit's modest ten kilometer per hour ground speed.

The Quirinus lacks an anti-personnel weapon mount, leaving troopers reliant on their primary armament and battle claw against unarmored infantry. This configuration favors engagements at range, where the David rifle and TAG coordination can soften targets before close contact becomes necessary.`,`StarCorp Industries developed the Quirinus at their Terra facility during the early Republic era, with the first production models entering service in 3093. Rather than restricting sales to the Republic Armed Forces, StarCorp marketed the suit broadly, finding eager buyers among the fractured provinces of the former Free Worlds League. The League's successor states, each maintaining independent military forces, welcomed a reliable medium battle armor that could fill generalist roles across varied operational environments.

Mercenary commands also proved enthusiastic customers, with some units accepting Quirinus suits as partial payment for contracts. The suit's combination of solid protection and a flexible weapon system made it attractive to organizations that needed adaptability without specialized logistics chains. By the mid-thirty-second century, the Quirinus had spread across much of the Inner Sphere's mercenary market and several provincial militaries, earning a quiet reputation as a dependable workhorse rather than a showpiece.`,{la:{manipulator:`battle-claw-vibro`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1},ra:{manipulator:`basic`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1}},{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-david-light-gauss-rifle`,`la`,`#0`],[`is-light-tag`,`body`,`#1`]]},`The Heavy Grenade Launcher configuration trades the long-range precision of the David rifle for flexible area-effect firepower at shorter distances. The launcher's ability to deliver explosive payloads across a wide engagement zone makes this configuration effective against clustered infantry, light vehicles, and entrenched positions. Paired with the suit's integral Light TAG designator, troopers can mark targets for supporting fire while simultaneously suppressing them with grenade bursts. Four hundred eighty kilograms of reactive armor absorb explosive impacts well, complementing the close-to-medium range role this configuration typically fills.

Jump jets allow ninety meters of leaping movement per burst, critical for closing distance and repositioning between volleys. The vibro battle claw provides a credible anti-BattleMech capability in swarming attacks, though the grenade launcher's limited ammunition supply demands careful fire discipline. Without an anti-personnel weapon mount, troopers must rely on the launcher's splash damage against soft targets.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-light-tag`,`body`,`#0`],[`is-heavy-grenade-launcher`,`la`,`#1`]]},`The Machine Gun configuration, introduced in 3138, adds a high-volume ballistic option to the Quirinus weapon family. The machine gun excels at suppressing infantry and engaging light targets with sustained fire, making this the most effective anti-personnel loadout available for the suit. The integral Light TAG designator complements the machine gun's suppressive role by allowing troopers to guide indirect fire onto hardened targets that resist small-arms fire. Reactive armor totaling four hundred eighty kilograms shields the trooper from explosive threats, though energy weapons bypass this protection entirely.

Jump capability of ninety meters per leap offsets the slow ground pace of ten kilometers per hour, enabling troopers to reposition quickly between engagements. The vibro battle claw retains the suit's anti-BattleMech swarming potential, giving this configuration a broader target profile than either the David or grenade launcher loadouts. Ammunition consumption remains the primary constraint during extended operations.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-machine-gun`,`la`,`#0`],[`is-light-tag`,`body`,`#1`]]},{manufacturer:`Luthien Armor Works|New Samarkand Metals`,primaryFactory:`Luthien|New Samarkand`},`The Raiden is a medium-weight battle armor developed by the Draconis Combine as an improvement on the Inner Sphere Standard design. Produced by Luthien Armor Works and New Samarkand Metals, the suit entered full production in 3055 after an intensive development program that began with captured Clan technology. Jump-capable and heavily armored for its weight class, the Raiden carries a modular weapon mount that accepts a range of infantry-scale weapons, along with a battle claw for anti-BattleMech operations and OmniMech transport. The suit became the preferred powered armor of the DCMS, earning a fearsome reputation across decades of service with the Dragon's infantry forces.`,`The anti-infantry configuration replaces the standard modular weapon mount with a fixed loadout optimized for close-quarters urban combat. Both a flamer and machine gun are mounted alongside an upgraded vibro-claw, giving the trooper devastating anti-personnel capability at short range. The vibro-claw's molecular-edge blade tears through conventional body armor and light structures with ease, making this variant particularly feared in building-to-building fighting. Jump jets retain the standard ninety-meter capability, allowing troopers to clear obstacles and gain rooftop positions during urban operations. The suit carries the same polymer-bonded armor as other production Raiden variants, providing heavy protection appropriate for the close engagements this configuration is designed to seek out. The dual-weapon approach sacrifices the flexibility of the modular mount for concentrated lethality against infantry and unarmored targets, making this a specialist configuration rather than a general-purpose loadout.`,`The Raiden traces its origins to the Battle of Wolcott in 3050, where overconfident Smoke Jaguar commanders lost twenty-four pristine Elemental battlesuits to Draconis Combine forces. The DCMS seized on this windfall and began reverse-engineering the captured technology almost immediately, with formal development commencing in November of that year.

The first prototype emerged in April 3052 and was presented to Coordinator Takashi Kurita in a live-fire demonstration that June. While the suit offered decent ground speed, jump capability, and respectable protection for its era, the Coordinator found it lacking. He directed the project team to redesign the suit so that a trooper could survive a direct hit from a large laser, and commanded the team's seventy-year-old director, Dr. Guthrie, to personally wear the prototype during its next trial.

Nine months of intensive work followed. The development team achieved the Coordinator's demanding armor threshold through a breakthrough in polymer bonding techniques, though the improvement required removing the suit's missile launcher. The trade-off proved worthwhile on multiple counts, as the removal also improved the suit's balance and handling characteristics. Dr. Guthrie donned the redesigned prototype for a live-fire exercise in February 3053. Lacking the physical conditioning or training to properly control the powered armor, the elderly scientist nonetheless marched onto the firing range and endured a barrage of laser and missile fire. The suit held together, barely, and Dr. Guthrie survived with five broken bones and a month-long hospital stay. The Coordinator rewarded his dedication with a promotion to head of the ISF's Research and Development department. When Dr. Guthrie passed away a year later, he was laid to rest with a full honor guard of Raiden-equipped infantrymen.

Full-scale production began in January 3055 at Luthien Armor Works, with New Samarkand Metals establishing a second production line shortly thereafter. The Raiden quickly became the common symbol of DCMS armored infantry, supplanting the Inner Sphere Standard in Combine service. Where the Standard had been a rushed effort to field any battle armor at all, the Raiden reflected the Combine's determination to produce a suit worthy of the Dragon's warriors. Its success encouraged further battle armor development within the DCMS, with the experiences and lessons learned from the Raiden program directly inspiring the lighter Kage reconnaissance suit and the heavier Kanazuchi assault armor.

The suit's modular weapon mount proved one of its most practical features, allowing field commanders to tailor loadouts to mission requirements without requiring factory modification. Additional weapon options expanded over time, with the Tsunami Heavy Gauss Rifle becoming available in 3056 and a compact medium-range missile launcher following in 3064. An urban combat variant mounting enhanced close-quarters weaponry entered service in 3058, initially as a field modification before transitioning to factory production.

Raiden-equipped infantry earned a fearsome reputation during the FedCom Civil War and the Jihad. Units such as the Dragonslayers of the Seventy-first Mechanized Infantry and the Soulsuckers of the Second Genyosha Infantry distinguished themselves repeatedly against both conventional forces and Word of Blake troops. The suit's combination of durability, mobility, and flexible armament ensured it remained a frontline asset for the DCMS well into the Dark Age, with both Luthien and New Samarkand continuing production runs across multiple decades.`,{la:{manipulator:`none`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1},ra:{manipulator:`battle-claw-vibro`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1}},{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-machine-gun`,`la`,`#0`],[`is-flamer-ba`,`ra`,`#1`]]},{manufacturer:`Luthien Armor Works`,primaryFactory:`Luthien`},`The original Raiden prototype mounts a fixed small laser in the right arm alongside a shoulder-mounted single-shot SRM launcher. This early configuration predates the modular weapon mount of the production model, carrying both weapons simultaneously rather than offering field-swappable options. The SRM launcher provides a one-use punch against hardened targets, after which the trooper relies on the small laser and battle claw for continued combat. Jump jets deliver ninety meters of leap capability, matching the Clan Elemental suit that inspired the design. The prototype's armor protection, while impressive for its era, falls short of the production model's polymer-bonded standard. The SRM launcher's weight and its impact on the suit's balance were among the factors that led to its removal during the redesign process, resulting in the heavier-armored production Raiden that entered service three years later.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`shots`,`oneShot`],$v:[[`is-small-laser`,`ra`,`#0`,null,null],[`is-srm-1`,`la`,`#1`,1,!0]]},`The flamer configuration mounts a battle armor-scale flamer in the suit's right-arm modular weapon mount. This loadout excels at clearing fortified positions and engaging conventional infantry, where the flamer's area-effect coverage compensates for its limited range. The left arm carries a battle claw that allows troopers to make anti-BattleMech attacks and ride OmniMechs into combat. Three jump jets provide ninety meters of jump capability, giving troopers the mobility to exploit vertical terrain and close rapidly with entrenched defenders. The suit's 450 kilograms of standard armor, achieved through an advanced polymer bonding process, can absorb a direct hit from a large pulse laser while keeping the trooper combat-effective. At one thousand kilograms total mass, the Raiden sits at the upper limit for mechanized battle armor operations, trading the lighter profile of scout suits for substantially greater protection and striking power.`,[{tag:`is-flamer-ba`,location:`ra`,uuid:`#0`}],`The laser configuration equips the Raiden's right-arm modular weapon mount with a small laser. This energy weapon provides reliable firepower without ammunition dependency, making it well-suited for extended operations and garrison duty where resupply may be limited. The small laser's accuracy at close range also makes it effective against other battle armor and light vehicles. As with all standard Raiden configurations, the suit retains its left-arm battle claw for anti-BattleMech attacks and OmniMech transport, three jump jets providing ninety meters of leap capability, and 450 kilograms of polymer-bonded armor protection. The laser variant's freedom from ammunition concerns comes at the cost of reduced anti-infantry effectiveness compared to area-effect weapons, making it less ideal for clearing entrenched conventional forces but more dependable across prolonged engagements.`,[{tag:`is-small-laser`,location:`ra`,uuid:`#0`}],`The machine gun configuration fits a battle armor-scale machine gun into the Raiden's right-arm modular weapon mount. The weapon delivers sustained ballistic fire effective against conventional infantry and soft targets, with ammunition capacity sufficient for extended engagements. Against hardened targets the machine gun offers less penetration than energy weapons, but its high rate of fire and suppressive capability make it a preferred choice for infantry support operations. The suit's standard combat systems remain unchanged from other Raiden configurations: a left-arm battle claw for anti-BattleMech attacks and OmniMech transport, three jump jets delivering ninety meters of jump capability, and 450 kilograms of polymer-bonded standard armor. The machine gun's reliance on ammunition introduces a logistical dependency absent in energy weapon loadouts, though DCMS quartermasters generally considered the trade-off acceptable given the weapon's raw effectiveness against unarmored targets.`,[{tag:`is-machine-gun`,location:`ra`,uuid:`#0`}],`The medium-range missile configuration mounts a compact single-tube MRM launcher with four reloads in the Raiden's right-arm modular weapon mount. Developed during the FedCom Civil War era and entering service in 3064, this loadout provides indirect fire capability rare among medium battle armor. The missile launcher's range advantage allows Raiden troopers to engage targets at distances where most battle armor weapons fall short, though the single-tube design limits its volume of fire per salvo. The suit otherwise retains its full combat suite, including jump jets for ninety-meter leaps, a left-arm battle claw enabling anti-BattleMech attacks and mechanized transport, and heavy polymer-bonded armor rated to withstand large energy weapon impacts. Ammunition dependency and the weapon's modest per-shot damage output represent the primary limitations of this configuration.`,[{tag:`is-mrm-1`,location:`ra`,uuid:`#0`,shots:4}],`The Tsunami configuration arms the Raiden with a Tsunami Heavy Gauss Rifle in its right-arm modular weapon mount. Available from 3056, this ballistic weapon delivers exceptional stopping power for a battle armor platform, capable of threatening light vehicles and other armored infantry at respectable ranges. The heavy gauss rifle's kinetic impact makes it one of the more powerful options available to Raiden troopers, though its ammunition supply is finite and the weapon adds considerable weight to the already heavy suit. Standard Raiden combat systems complement the Tsunami's firepower: jump jets provide ninety meters of mobility, a battle claw on the left arm supports anti-BattleMech operations and OmniMech mounting, and the suit's advanced polymer-bonded armor absorbs significant punishment before failing. The Tsunami variant fills a fire support role, engaging harder targets that lighter weapon loadouts struggle to damage.`,[{tag:`is-tsunami-gauss-rifle`,location:`ra`,uuid:`#0`}],{manufacturer:`New Samarkand Metals`,primaryFactory:`New Samarkand`},`The Raiden II is a Dark Age-era upgrade of the Draconis Combine's long-serving Raiden battle armor. Produced by New Samarkand Metals, the suit features upgraded armor protection and an additional weapon mount, addressing limitations identified through decades of operational experience with the original design. The upgrade retains the Raiden's proven jump capability and battle claw, maintaining compatibility with established DCMS infantry doctrine. Introduced around 3123, the Raiden II gave Combine armored infantry improved survivability without requiring a complete redesign of the familiar Raiden platform.`,`The Raiden II's flamer configuration carries a battle armor-scale flamer as its primary weapon, paired with the anti-personnel weapon mount that distinguishes the Raiden II from its predecessor. The suit's most significant upgrade is its reflective armor, which disperses energy weapon impacts more effectively than the original Raiden's polymer-bonded standard plating. Against laser-heavy opponents this protection gives Raiden II troopers a meaningful survivability advantage, though the reflective coating offers reduced protection against ballistic and explosive weapons compared to conventional armor of equivalent weight. Jump jets maintain the ninety-meter leap capability inherited from the original Raiden, and the left-arm battle claw retains full anti-BattleMech attack and OmniMech transport functionality. The flamer's area-effect fire combined with the anti-personnel mount makes this configuration effective for clearing infantry positions, particularly in environments where energy weapons are the primary threat.`,`The Raiden II emerged from the Draconis Combine's growing embrace of reflective armor technology during the early thirty-second century. The Kishi battle armor had demonstrated the value of reflective plating against energy-heavy opponents, and DCMS procurement officers quickly authorized a similar upgrade for the long-serving Raiden chassis. New Samarkand Metals received the contract, producing an updated suit that replaced the standard polymer-bonded armor with reflective plating while adding an anti-personnel weapon mount absent from the original design. The Raiden II entered production around 3123 and saw deployment with frontline DCMS formations during the turbulent Dark Age period. While not a radical redesign, the upgrade ensured that Combine battle armor infantry retained an edge against energy-weapon-equipped opponents, extending the Raiden platform's service life well beyond what its original designers likely envisioned.`,[{tag:`is-flamer-ba`,location:`la`,uuid:`#0`}],`The heavy machine gun configuration arms the Raiden II with a weapon offering greater penetration and stopping power than the standard machine gun available to the original Raiden. The heavy machine gun's increased caliber makes it effective against lightly armored vehicles and battle armor in addition to conventional infantry, broadening the suit's engagement envelope. Like the flamer variant, this configuration benefits from the Raiden II's reflective armor, which provides enhanced resistance to energy weapons at the cost of reduced protection against ballistic impacts. The anti-personnel weapon mount adds a secondary close-range option absent from original Raiden designs. Standard mobility systems carry over from the base Raiden platform, with jump jets providing ninety meters of leap capability and a battle claw enabling anti-BattleMech attacks and mechanized transport. The heavy machine gun's ammunition demands require careful supply management, but its hitting power makes the trade-off attractive for Combine infantry commanders.`,[{tag:`is-heavy-machine-gun`,location:`la`,uuid:`#0`}],{manufacturer:`Marian Arms Inc.`,primaryFactory:`Horatius`},`The Ravager is an assault-class battle armor produced by Marian Arms Incorporated on Horatius in the Marian Hegemony. Introduced in 3084, the suit was designed using common Inner Sphere technology to fill both a domestic defense role and generate export revenue for the small Periphery nation. The Ravager gained rapid popularity among mercenary commands and smaller states, earning a reputation as a rugged and affordable heavy combat suit. While widely acknowledged as one of the least attractive battle armor designs in production, the combination of heavy protection and an accessible price point has ensured steady demand across the Inner Sphere and beyond.`,`The Ravager traces its origins to the brief alliance between the Marian Hegemony and the Word of Blake. During the Jihad, the Blakists had begun upgrading the Marian Arms facility on Horatius, including construction of a battle armor production line. When Caesar Julius O'Reilly evicted the Word from his nation, the partially completed facility sat dormant for years. After the Jihad, Doctor Dafyyd Rou, a former employee of Irian Technologies, fled into Marian space. Caesar Cassius O'Reilly tasked Rou with completing the facility and producing a new suit for both Hegemony defense and export sales. The development program nearly bankrupted the small Periphery nation, but Rou delivered a functional assault suit by 3084.

Within a year the Ravager had become the most sought-after battle armor on the open markets. The Marian government sold the suit to any willing buyer, channeling profits back into the Horatius factory and further research. Mercenary commands and smaller states proved eager customers, with independent nations in the former Free Worlds League showing particular interest. An upgraded variant with indirect fire capability entered production around 3101, sustaining export demand well into the Dark Age.`,`is-light-recoilless-rifle`,`The Ravager is a two-ton assault suit built with common Inner Sphere technology and modified BattleMech armor plating. Its primary weapon is a multi-barrel heavy recoilless rifle mounted on the right arm, featuring redundant barrels that allow continued operation even after sustaining damage. Two single-tube rocket launchers on the main body provide supplemental firepower, while a battle claw on the left arm handles close-quarters work. Protection is the suit's defining characteristic, with nearly a full metric ton of standard armor giving operators confidence against all but the heaviest autocannon fire. Ground speed reaches a respectable twenty kilometers per hour, quick for a suit of this weight class.

The Ravager lacks jump capability entirely, limiting its tactical mobility in rough terrain. Many observers consider the overall firepower modest for an assault-class design, and the suit has earned a reputation as one of the least visually appealing battle armor in service. Operators have taken to calling it "the Waddle," though most are willing to trade aesthetics for the survivability it offers.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`is-heavy-recoilless-rifle`,`ra`,`#0`],[`is-rocket-launcher-1`,`body`,`#1`],[`is-rocket-launcher-1`,`body`,`#2`]]},{manufacturer:`Clan Cloud Cobra|Olivetti Weapons`,primaryFactory:`Homer|Sudeten`},`The Resgate is a Clan-manufactured light exoskeleton designed exclusively for search and rescue operations. Developed by Clan Cloud Cobra's scientist caste in the early thirtieth century, the suit was built to improve emergency response capabilities across Cobra-held worlds. Carrying no weapons, the Resgate prioritized sensor capability, debris-clearing tools, and operator protection over any form of combat effectiveness. The design earned a strong reputation among emergency services personnel for its reliability under harsh and dangerous field conditions.`,`The Interdictor configuration replaces the Resgate's sensor suite and salvage arm with an electronic countermeasures package and a pair of armored gloves. Developed during the Jihad, this variant shifts the exoskeleton's role from rescue operations to electronic warfare support. The ECM suite disrupts enemy targeting systems and communications within its effective radius, providing valuable cover for friendly forces operating nearby.

Armored gloves replace the specialized manipulators of the base model, sacrificing debris-clearing capability for improved manual dexterity in field conditions. The suit retains its fire-resistant armor and jump jets, maintaining the mobility and thermal protection of the original design. Without sensors or salvage equipment, the Interdictor cannot perform rescue operations. It fills a niche role as a lightweight, mobile ECM platform that can accompany infantry formations into terrain too restrictive for larger electronic warfare vehicles.`,`In the late thirtieth century, a massive earthquake struck the Cloud Cobra enclave on Homer, killing over eight hundred merchant and laborer caste members and devastating a quarter of the settlement. The Warrior Council, shaken by the scale of the disaster, directed the scientist caste to develop improved emergency response tools. Working from castoff battle armor prototypes, Cloud Cobra scientists produced a compact light exoskeleton stripped down to the essentials needed for locating and extracting trapped or injured civilians.

The suit's protective capabilities expanded following a trial between Cloud Cobra and Clan Fire Mandrill scientists. Scientist Carl of the Cloud Cobras won access to fire-resistant armor technology through a magnetic belt experiment, and the material was quickly integrated into the Resgate design. The 849th Fire Response Unit validated the upgrade when the DropShip Dresden crashed on Babylon. Resgate operators pulled nearly the entire crew from the burning wreckage, losing only a single life.

During the Jihad, Cloud Cobra engineers developed four specialized variants that expanded the exoskeleton's role beyond rescue operations into electronic warfare, reconnaissance, target designation, and fire support. Production later spread to Olivetti Weaponry on Sudeten under Clan Jade Falcon control.`,[{tag:`clan-ecm-suite`,location:`body`,uuid:`#0`}],`The Scout configuration retains the improved sensor suite of the base Resgate while replacing the salvage arm with armored gloves. This variant preserves the exoskeleton's reconnaissance capability while providing better manual dexterity for field operations. The sensor suite maintains its ability to detect life signs and gather environmental data, making the Scout useful for both forward reconnaissance and search operations.

Armored gloves give the operator improved grip and handling compared to the salvage arm, allowing for more versatile fieldwork. The suit keeps its fire-resistant armor and jump jets, offering the same thermal protection and mobility as the standard model. Without the salvage arm, the Scout cannot efficiently clear heavy debris, limiting its effectiveness in pure rescue scenarios. The configuration works best as a forward observer or pathfinder, scouting ahead of rescue teams to locate survivors and assess conditions before committing heavier equipment.`,[{tag:`clan-improved-sensors`,location:`body`,uuid:`#0`}],`The Spotter configuration strips the Resgate's standard equipment in favor of a light TAG designator and armored gloves. This Jihad-era variant transforms the rescue exoskeleton into a forward targeting platform, capable of painting targets for artillery and missile strikes. The light TAG unit allows the operator to designate targets for semi-guided munitions from positions that heavier spotting platforms cannot reach.

Armored gloves provide adequate manual dexterity for field operations. The suit retains its fire-resistant armor and jump capability, giving it the thermal protection and mobility to operate in forward positions. Without sensors, the Spotter relies entirely on the operator's own situational awareness to locate and designate targets. The configuration sacrifices all rescue functionality for a single combat-support role, making it the most specialized of the Resgate variants.`,[{tag:`clan-light-tag`,location:`body`,uuid:`#0`}],`The Resgate weighs four hundred kilograms and carries no weapons of any kind, reflecting its single-minded focus on emergency rescue. Its primary tool is an improved sensor suite capable of detecting life signs through up to ten meters of dense material, paired with a salvage arm on the left limb for clearing debris and extracting trapped personnel. A basic manipulator on the right arm handles more delicate tasks. Jump jets provide the mobility needed to reach disaster sites that ground vehicles cannot access, giving the suit a ninety-meter jump range at a ground speed of thirty kilometers per hour.

Fire-resistant armor protects the operator from intense heat for limited periods, a critical advantage during structural fires and vehicle crashes. The suit lacks any form of offensive or defensive weaponry, and its light construction offers minimal ballistic protection. Operators working in active combat zones require escort from armed units.`,`The Support configuration replaces all standard rescue equipment with a light machine gun and armored gloves, converting the Resgate into a light combat platform. This Jihad-era variant is the only configuration in the family that carries offensive weaponry. The light machine gun provides effective anti-infantry firepower, giving the Support variant the ability to engage unarmored targets and provide suppressive fire for friendly formations.

Armored gloves offer adequate handling for field operations and close-quarters situations. Fire-resistant armor and jump jets carry over from the base design, maintaining thermal protection and tactical mobility. The Support variant's combat capability remains modest by battle armor standards. Its light machine gun handles conventional infantry and soft targets effectively but struggles against armored opponents. The configuration fills a security role, providing armed escort for rescue teams operating in contested areas where unarmed exoskeletons would be vulnerable.`,[{tag:`clan-light-machine-gun`,location:`ra`,uuid:`#0`}],{manufacturer:`BA Facility Gamma`,primaryFactory:`Mannedorf`},`The Rogue Bear is a Clan Ghost Bear heavy battlesuit produced at BA Facility Gamma on Mannedorf. First fielded in 3073 during the closing years of the Jihad, the design fills a rapid-response role within garrison and second-line formations, offering jump capability and fixed armament suited to patrol and security operations. Weighing 1,500 kilograms, the Rogue Bear earned a reputation for reliability and low maintenance requirements, qualities that proved valuable during a period of significant logistical strain across the Ghost Bear Dominion. Several derivative designs have since been developed from the Rogue Bear chassis.`,`The Hybrid is a stealth-focused variant refined from the experimental HR prototype. Engineers replaced the HR's partial wing system with a jump booster providing equivalent ninety-meter range but greater reliability. Battle claws gave way to basic manipulators, freeing enough weight to double the suit's secondary armament to two machine guns and two anti-personnel weapon mounts. The Hybrid retains basic stealth armor and a camouflage system, granting reduced detection signatures at the cost of lighter protection compared to the standard Rogue Bear.

This creates a suit caught between roles: its weapons encourage close-range engagement while its stealth systems reward standoff distance. The blending of Inner Sphere and Clan technologies introduces maintenance complexity despite the suit's Easy to Maintain design, as non-standard parts can be difficult to source. Formations already operating Kobold suits have adopted the Hybrid most readily, benefiting from parts commonality with the Rogue Bear chassis.`,`The Rogue Bear emerged from BA Facility Gamma on Mannedorf during one of the Ghost Bear Dominion's most turbulent periods. First observed in garrison forces during December 3073, the heavy battlesuit drew immediate comparisons to the Golem. Careful analysis confirmed it as an entirely new design, built along similar lines but lighter and with a different operational emphasis. The suit entered service with KungsArmé formations absorbed into the Ghost Bear touman, with the recently activated First Tyr Assault Cluster among its earliest recipients. The First Tyr, stationed on the former Free Rasalhague Republic capital of Orestes, was primarily composed of ethnic Rasalhagians retrained in Clan organization and tactics.

Following a brutal engagement against Word of Blake forces on Radstadt in 3074, Ghost Bear commanders rewarded the First Tyr's abtakha troopers with early production Rogue Bear suits. BA Facility Gamma continued production through the Jihad and into the Dark Age, with the Rasalhague Dominion fielding the design across multiple Galaxies. The 283rd Battle Cluster, also stationed on Mannedorf, maintained strong contingents and employed them effectively against Draconis Combine border raids. The chassis proved adaptable, spawning the Constable Pacification Suit and the Wraith battle armor, which was originally prototyped as a Rogue Bear variant.`,{$k:[`tag`],$v:[[`quirk-easy-to-maintain`],[`quirk-non-standard-parts`]]},{la:{manipulator:`basic`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1},ra:{manipulator:`basic`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1}},{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`],$v:[[`clan-jump-booster`,`body`,`#0`],[`is-camo-system`,`body`,`#1`],[`clan-machine-gun`,`la`,`#2`],[`clan-machine-gun`,`ra`,`#3`]]},{$k:[`location`,`weapon`],$v:[[`la`,`inf-auto-rifle`],[`ra`,`inf-auto-rifle`]]},`The Rogue Bear is a heavy battlesuit weighing 1,500 kilograms, built around a fixed weapons loadout with no disposable components. Its primary anti-vehicle armament is a three-tube short-range missile launcher hardwired to its back, carrying four reloads. Two arm-mounted machine guns provide effective anti-infantry firepower. For close combat and OmniMech transport capability, the suit employs vibro-blade-enhanced battle claw manipulators. Jump jets allow sixty-meter leaps under terrestrial gravity.

The suit carries enough standard armor to nearly absorb a Gauss rifle hit, though this protection comes at the cost of reduced sophistication compared to heavier assault designs. The emphasis on standard, non-disposable components reflects a practical design philosophy, reducing logistical strain on garrison formations operating far from primary supply lines. The fixed missile launcher does add bulk even when ammunition is expended, limiting the suit's utility in prolonged engagements where weight becomes a factor.`,{la:{manipulator:`battle-claw-vibro`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1},ra:{manipulator:`battle-claw-vibro`,adaptor:!1,cargoHalfTons:1}},{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`shots`],$v:[[`clan-machine-gun`,`la`,`#0`,null],[`clan-machine-gun`,`ra`,`#1`,null],[`clan-srm-3`,`body`,`#2`,4]]},`The Upgrade configuration trades raw firepower for improved survivability. Engineers reduced the missile launcher to a two-tube system while retaining its four-shot ammunition supply, and replaced the original vibro-blade manipulators with heavy battle claws. The freed weight and volume allowed installation of reactive armor plating across the suit's frame, providing significantly better protection against missile and ballistic threats than the standard armor of the original design.

The heavy battle claws retain OmniMech transport capability while offering strong melee performance, though they lack the cutting precision of vibro-blades. The twin machine guns carry over unchanged, maintaining the suit's anti-infantry effectiveness. This configuration shifts the Rogue Bear toward a more defensive role, better suited to holding positions and absorbing fire during close-quarters engagements where the reactive armor's properties are most effective.`,{$k:[`tag`,`location`,`uuid`,`shots`],$v:[[`clan-machine-gun`,`la`,`#0`,null],[`clan-machine-gun`,`ra`,`#1`,null],[`clan-srm-2`,`body`,`#2`,4]]}],designs:JSON.parse(`{"450":{"tro":{"production":"~0","text":{"overview":"~1","capabilities":"~2","deployment":"Five-trooper Kopis Mk IIr Points appeared primarily among Clan forces operating in former Republic space, where battlefield salvage provided a steady supply of the reactive-armored suits. The enhanced armor protection made the slow-moving Kopis more viable in open-field engagements where Clan warriors would otherwise dismiss the design's limited mobility.","battleHistory":"~3"}},"name":"Kopis Assault Battle Armor (AI Mk IIr)(Sqd5)","weightClass":"assault","arms":"~4","armor":"ba-reactive","armorPoints":10,"items":"~5","squadSize":5,"era":"late-rep"},"451":{"tro":{"production":"~0","text":{"overview":"~1","capabilities":"~2","deployment":"Republic and successor-state forces organized Mk IIr suits into six-trooper Level I formations, where the reactive armor's protection multiplied across the larger squad size to create formations with exceptional durability against ballistic and missile threats. These formations were frequently assigned to garrison duty on contested border worlds.","battleHistory":"~3"}},"name":"Kopis Assault Battle Armor (AI Mk IIr)(Sqd6)","weightClass":"assault","arms":"~4","armor":"ba-reactive","armorPoints":10,"items":"~5","squadSize":6,"era":"late-rep"},"452":{"tro":{"production":"~0","text":{"overview":"~1","capabilities":"~6","deployment":"Inner Sphere forces deployed four-trooper Anti-Infantry squads specifically for urban clearance operations during the Liberation of Terra. These squads worked in tight coordination, with troopers alternating flamer and pulse laser fire to systematically clear buildings and fortifications held by Word of Blake infantry.","battleHistory":"~3"}},"name":"Kopis Assault Battle Armor (Anti-Infantry)(Sqd4)","weightClass":"assault","arms":"~4","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":10,"items":"~7","apMounts":"~8","era":"jihad"},"453":{"tro":{"production":"~0","text":{"overview":"~1","capabilities":"~6","deployment":"Clan warriors who captured Anti-Infantry Kopis suits organized them into five-trooper Points. The configuration's close-quarters focus limited its tactical utility for Clan forces accustomed to ranged engagement doctrines, though some garrison Points employed the suits for facility defense and boarding actions where the flamer proved effective in confined spaces.","battleHistory":"~3"}},"name":"Kopis Assault Battle Armor (Anti-Infantry)(Sqd5)","weightClass":"assault","arms":"~4","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":10,"items":"~7","apMounts":"~8","squadSize":5,"era":"jihad"},"454":{"tro":{"production":"~0","text":{"overview":"~1","capabilities":"~6","deployment":"Coalition forces at the Liberation of Terra organized some Anti-Infantry Kopis suits into six-trooper Level I formations, particularly among units with ComStar organizational heritage. The larger squad size proved advantageous in building-by-building fighting, where additional troopers provided better coverage of multiple entry points during clearance operations.","battleHistory":"~3"}},"name":"Kopis Assault Battle Armor (Anti-Infantry)(Sqd6)","weightClass":"assault","arms":"~4","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":10,"items":"~7","apMounts":"~8","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"455":{"tro":{"production":"~0","text":{"overview":"~1","capabilities":"~9","deployment":"Four-trooper Mortar squads served as organic indirect fire support for Oriente Province infantry formations. Commanders positioned these squads behind cover or on elevated terrain, where they could deliver heavy mortar rounds onto enemy positions while relying on reactive armor to survive counter-battery fire.","battleHistory":"~3"}},"name":"Kopis Assault Battle Armor (Mortar)(Sqd4)","weightClass":"assault","arms":"~4","armor":"ba-reactive","armorPoints":10,"items":"~10","apMounts":"~8","era":"late-rep"},"456":{"tro":{"production":"~0","text":{"overview":"~1","capabilities":"~9","deployment":"Clan forces that acquired Mortar-variant Kopis suits organized them into five-trooper Points, though the configuration's indirect fire role was an unusual fit for Clan battle doctrine. Some garrison clusters employed Mortar Points as area denial assets, using sustained mortar fire to channel enemy advances into predetermined engagement zones.","battleHistory":"~3"}},"name":"Kopis Assault Battle Armor (Mortar)(Sqd5)","weightClass":"assault","arms":"~4","armor":"ba-reactive","armorPoints":10,"items":"~10","apMounts":"~8","squadSize":5,"era":"late-rep"},"457":{"tro":{"production":"~0","text":{"overview":"~1","capabilities":"~9","deployment":"Six-trooper Mortar Level I formations provided concentrated indirect fire support for Republic and successor-state combined arms formations. The larger squad size allowed these formations to sustain longer barrages, with troopers cycling fire to maintain continuous coverage of target areas during extended engagements.","battleHistory":"~3"}},"name":"Kopis Assault Battle Armor (Mortar)(Sqd6)","weightClass":"assault","arms":"~4","armor":"ba-reactive","armorPoints":10,"items":"~10","apMounts":"~8","squadSize":6,"era":"late-rep"},"458":{"tro":{"production":"~11","text":{"overview":"~12","capabilities":"~13","deployment":"Federated Suns emergency response teams organized Krise operators into four-member squads, the standard Inner Sphere infantry formation size. These squads deployed across the Suns in civilian disaster relief roles, responding to building collapses, industrial accidents, and natural catastrophes. The suit's ease of operation allowed rapid training of emergency personnel who lacked formal powered armor experience.","battleHistory":"~14"}},"quirks":"~15","name":"Krise PA(L) (Sqd4)","weightClass":"pa-l","groundMP":3,"motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~16","armorPoints":1,"items":"~17","era":"civil-war"},"459":{"tro":{"production":"~11","text":{"overview":"~12","capabilities":"~13","deployment":"Clan organizations that obtained Krise exoskeletons organized them into five-member Points consistent with standard Clan doctrine. The suit's simple controls and civilian focus made it accessible even to lower-caste operators, and Points of Krise-equipped personnel supplemented dedicated Clan rescue assets during large-scale emergencies on occupied worlds.","battleHistory":"~14"}},"quirks":"~15","name":"Krise PA(L) (Sqd5)","weightClass":"pa-l","groundMP":3,"motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~16","armorPoints":1,"items":"~17","squadSize":5,"era":"civil-war"},"460":{"tro":{"production":"~11","text":{"overview":"~12","capabilities":"~13","deployment":"ComStar and Word of Blake formations fielded the Krise in six-member Level I teams for disaster response and humanitarian operations. The larger team size provided additional manpower for complex rescue scenarios, and the suit's extended life support capability proved particularly valuable during prolonged operations in contaminated environments.","battleHistory":"~14"}},"quirks":"~15","name":"Krise PA(L) (Sqd6)","weightClass":"pa-l","groundMP":3,"motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~16","armorPoints":1,"items":"~17","squadSize":6,"era":"civil-war"},"461":{"tro":{"production":"~18","text":{"overview":"~19","capabilities":"~20","deployment":"Five-trooper Points of Magnetic Longinus suits appeared in garrison formations that combined Clan-style organization with the practical need to transport battle armor on conventional platforms. The magnetic clamps removed the OmniMech dependency that normally constrained battle armor deployment, allowing Points to attach to any available BattleMech for rapid battlefield repositioning.","battleHistory":"~21"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor (Magnetic)(Sqd5)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~22","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":9,"items":"~23","apMounts":"~24","squadSize":5,"era":"jihad"},"462":{"tro":{"production":"~18","text":{"overview":"~19","capabilities":"~20","deployment":"Word of Blake Militia Level I sections equipped with the Magnetic variant leveraged the larger formation size to overwhelm dismounting defenses. Six troopers riding into battle on the hulls of supporting BattleMechs could detach simultaneously and swarm a target from multiple angles, a tactic that Blakist commanders employed to devastating effect during Protectorate security operations.","battleHistory":"~21"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor (Magnetic)(Sqd6)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~22","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":9,"items":"~23","apMounts":"~24","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"463":{"tro":{"production":"~25","text":{"overview":"~19","capabilities":"~26","deployment":"When the \\"David\\" light Gauss rifle became available in 3063, FWLM commanders quickly adopted the configuration for squads tasked with open-terrain operations. Four-trooper squads equipped with the ranged weapon could engage targets at distances where other Longinus loadouts would need to close, giving FWLM battle armor a standoff capability it previously lacked. David-armed squads saw particular use in the rolling plains and open farmland common to many League border worlds.","battleHistory":"~21"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor [David](Sqd4)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~27","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":9,"items":"~28","apMounts":"~24","era":"civil-war"},"464":{"tro":{"production":"~25","text":{"overview":"~19","capabilities":"~26","deployment":"Five-trooper Points fielding the David light Gauss rifle configuration operated with forces accustomed to Clan organizational patterns. The ranged engagement capability suited the Clan preference for opening engagements at distance before closing to finish, and Points equipped with the David rifle could contribute meaningful firepower in the early phases of a skirmish without exposing themselves to return fire.","battleHistory":"~21"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor [David](Sqd5)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~27","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":9,"items":"~28","apMounts":"~24","squadSize":5,"era":"civil-war"},"465":{"tro":{"production":"~25","text":{"overview":"~19","capabilities":"~26","deployment":"Word of Blake Militia Level I formations equipped with David-armed Longinus suits provided long-range fire support within Blakist garrison commands. The six troopers' combined light Gauss fire gave these sections a ranged punch that complemented heavier BattleMech and vehicle assets, allowing Militia commanders to extend their engagement envelope without committing armored units forward.","battleHistory":"~21"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor [David](Sqd6)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~22","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":9,"items":"~28","apMounts":"~24","squadSize":6,"era":"civil-war"},"466":{"tro":{"production":"~25","text":{"overview":"~19","capabilities":"~29","deployment":"Free Worlds League Military squads equipped with flamer-armed Longinus suits frequently drew assignments in urban pacification and defensive clearing operations. Four-trooper squads proved well-suited for sweeping corridors and flushing entrenched infantry from hardened positions, where the flamer's area suppression compensated for its limited range. FWLM commanders often paired these squads with conventional infantry platoons to secure contested buildings floor by floor.","battleHistory":"~21"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor [Flamer](Sqd4)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~22","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":9,"items":"~30","apMounts":"~24","era":"clan-inv"},"467":{"tro":{"production":"~25","text":{"overview":"~19","capabilities":"~29","deployment":"Five-trooper Points of flamer-equipped Longinus suits saw limited service with Clan-affiliated forces that acquired the design through battlefield salvage or trade. Wolf's Dragoons organized captured suits into standard Clan Points, employing them in garrison actions where their close-range firepower proved effective at area denial and perimeter clearing.","battleHistory":"~21"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor [Flamer](Sqd5)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~22","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":9,"items":"~30","apMounts":"~24","squadSize":5,"era":"clan-inv"},"468":{"tro":{"production":"~25","text":{"overview":"~19","capabilities":"~29","deployment":"Word of Blake Militia formations organized Longinus suits into six-trooper Level I sections following the Captain-General's decision to supply the design. Flamer-armed Level I units excelled in the close-quarters fighting that characterized Blakist urban operations, and their larger formation size allowed Militia commanders to saturate a defensive position more quickly than the four-trooper FWLM squads they trained alongside.","battleHistory":"~21"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor [Flamer](Sqd6)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~22","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":9,"items":"~30","apMounts":"~24","squadSize":6,"era":"clan-inv"},"469":{"tro":{"production":"~25","text":{"overview":"~19","capabilities":"~31","deployment":"The laser configuration became the preferred loadout for FWLM squads assigned to extended patrol and garrison duty, where the absence of ammunition dependency reduced logistical strain. Four-trooper squads operating along the League's extensive border regions could sustain operations for days without resupply, making the laser-armed Longinus a practical choice for frontier commands stretched thin across multiple systems.","battleHistory":"~21"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor [Laser](Sqd4)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~22","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":9,"items":"~32","apMounts":"~24","era":"clan-inv"},"470":{"tro":{"production":"~25","text":{"overview":"~19","capabilities":"~31","deployment":"Laser-equipped Longinus suits organized into five-trooper Points appeared among Clan-affiliated garrison forces that valued the loadout's endurance. Without the need for ammunition resupply, Points operating in remote postings or aboard WarShips could maintain readiness indefinitely, an advantage that suited the self-sufficient operational style of Clan second-line formations.","battleHistory":"~21"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor [Laser](Sqd5)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~22","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":9,"items":"~32","apMounts":"~24","squadSize":5,"era":"clan-inv"},"471":{"tro":{"production":"~25","text":{"overview":"~19","capabilities":"~31","deployment":"Word of Blake Militia Level I sections fielding laser-armed Longinus suits served as standing garrison elements on Protectorate worlds. The six-trooper formation provided enough firepower to discourage raiding parties and secure key installations without ammunition constraints, freeing Blakist supply chains to prioritize heavier frontline formations during the Jihad.","battleHistory":"~21"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor [Laser](Sqd6)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~22","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":9,"items":"~32","apMounts":"~24","squadSize":6,"era":"clan-inv"},"472":{"tro":{"production":"~25","text":{"overview":"~19","capabilities":"~33","deployment":"Machine gun-armed Longinus squads filled a straightforward anti-infantry role within FWLM formations. Four-trooper squads assigned to rear-area security and convoy escort duties used the sustained automatic fire to suppress unarmored threats quickly, though ammunition limitations meant these squads required closer logistical support than energy-armed alternatives. The MG loadout remained common among garrison units that prioritized volume of fire over operational endurance.","battleHistory":"~21"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor [MG](Sqd4)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~22","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":9,"items":"~34","apMounts":"~24","era":"clan-inv"},"473":{"tro":{"production":"~25","text":{"overview":"~19","capabilities":"~33","deployment":"Five-trooper Points carrying machine gun-equipped Longinus suits served in secondary garrison roles with forces that adopted Clan organizational structure. The automatic fire capability gave these Points effective anti-infantry suppression, though the ammunition dependency made them less popular than laser-armed alternatives for extended independent operations.","battleHistory":"~21"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor [MG](Sqd5)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~22","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":9,"items":"~34","apMounts":"~24","squadSize":5,"era":"clan-inv"},"474":{"tro":{"production":"~25","text":{"overview":"~19","capabilities":"~33","deployment":"Word of Blake Militia Level I formations armed with machine gun Longinus suits provided base defense and installation security across the Protectorate. The six-trooper formation's combined automatic fire could shred unarmored infantry formations at close range, making these sections effective deterrents against conventional ground assault. Militia commanders often positioned them at chokepoints and access corridors where the volume of fire compensated for limited range.","battleHistory":"~21"}},"name":"Longinus Battle Armor [MG](Sqd6)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~22","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":9,"items":"~34","apMounts":"~24","squadSize":6,"era":"clan-inv"},"475":{"tro":{"production":"~35","text":{"overview":"~36","capabilities":"~37","deployment":"Marian Hegemony legions field the Marauder in four-trooper squads, the standard formation size for Inner Sphere battle armor. Marian commanders typically employ squads in a fire support role, using the suit's target designation capability to call in indirect fire while engaging at range. Taurian militia units and Periphery mercenary commands also operate the suit in this configuration.","battleHistory":"~38"}},"quirks":"~39","name":"Marauder Battle Armor (Sqd4)","weightClass":"heavy","armorPoints":9,"items":"~40","apMounts":"~8","era":"late-rep"},"476":{"tro":{"production":"~35","text":{"overview":"~36","capabilities":"~37","deployment":"Five-trooper Points of Marauders appear primarily among mercenary commands that have adopted Clan-style organizational structures. The additional trooper per formation increases the volume of fire a single Point can deliver, making the configuration attractive to commands that favor concentrated firepower over broader coverage.","battleHistory":"~38"}},"quirks":"~39","name":"Marauder Battle Armor (Sqd5)","weightClass":"heavy","armorPoints":9,"items":"~40","apMounts":"~8","squadSize":5,"era":"late-rep"},"477":{"tro":{"production":"~35","text":{"overview":"~36","capabilities":"~37","deployment":"Six-trooper formations following the Level I organizational model have appeared among commands with ties to former ComStar or Word of Blake military traditions. The larger formation size compensates somewhat for the suit's limited mobility by providing additional firepower per tactical element, though the logistical burden of maintaining six suits per formation can strain smaller units.","battleHistory":"~38"}},"quirks":"~39","name":"Marauder Battle Armor (Sqd6)","weightClass":"heavy","armorPoints":9,"items":"~40","apMounts":"~8","squadSize":6,"era":"late-rep"},"478":{"tro":{"production":"~41","text":{"overview":"~42","capabilities":"~43","deployment":"Captured Nephilim suits equipped with tasers and LRM launchers were among the most closely studied Demon configurations. Four-trooper squads provided allied weapons researchers with insight into Blakist taser technology and the detachable weapon pack system, both of which influenced post-war battle armor development programs across multiple Inner Sphere states.","battleHistory":"~44"}},"name":"Nephilim Assault Battle Armor (Capture Team)[HMG](Sqd4)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~45","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":16,"items":"~46","era":"jihad"},"479":{"tro":{"production":"~41","text":{"overview":"~42","capabilities":"~43","deployment":"The capture team concept held no appeal for Clan warriors whose honor codes demanded the destruction of enemies in combat. Any Nephilim capture suits salvaged by Clan forces and organized into five-trooper Points would almost certainly have been stripped of their taser systems and reconfigured for direct combat roles.","battleHistory":"~44"}},"name":"Nephilim Assault Battle Armor (Capture Team)[HMG](Sqd5)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~45","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":16,"items":"~46","squadSize":5,"era":"jihad"},"480":{"tro":{"production":"~41","text":{"overview":"~42","capabilities":"~43","deployment":"Capture team Nephilim troopers deployed in six-trooper Level I formations as specialized seizure units under direct Manei Domini authority. A full Level I could potentially disable multiple BattleMechs and vehicles in rapid succession, seizing both equipment and personnel for the Word's intelligence apparatus. The LRM launcher provided stand-off firepower during the approach phase, while the heavy machine gun sustained the engagement after the tasers were discharged or jettisoned.","battleHistory":"~44"}},"name":"Nephilim Assault Battle Armor (Capture Team)[HMG](Sqd6)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~45","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":16,"items":"~46","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"481":{"tro":{"production":"~41","text":{"overview":"~42","capabilities":"~47","deployment":"The Gauss Nephilim's precision ballistic weapons suite made it one of the more studied Demon configurations in allied evaluation programs. Four-trooper squads equipped with the triple-Gauss loadout provided valuable data on Blakist miniaturized Gauss technology, particularly the Magshot rifles that duplicated Federated Suns designs using Word of Blake manufacturing processes.","battleHistory":"~44"}},"name":"Nephilim Assault Battle Armor (Gauss)(Sqd4)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~45","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":16,"items":"~48","era":"jihad"},"482":{"tro":{"production":"~41","text":{"overview":"~42","capabilities":"~47","deployment":"Clan scientists would have found the Gauss Nephilim's weapons technology of considerable interest, as the miniaturized Gauss systems represented engineering approaches different from Clan designs. Any salvaged suits organized into five-trooper Points would likely have been directed to research facilities rather than combat formations.","battleHistory":"~44"}},"name":"Nephilim Assault Battle Armor (Gauss)(Sqd5)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~45","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":16,"items":"~48","squadSize":5,"era":"jihad"},"483":{"tro":{"production":"~41","text":{"overview":"~42","capabilities":"~47","deployment":"Gauss-armed Nephilim troopers operated in six-trooper Level I formations as precision fire support. The all-ballistic weapons suite allowed sustained engagement without heat concerns, and the cybernetically enhanced Manei Domini operators could exploit the weapons' accuracy to devastating effect. This configuration was reportedly favored by the Domini elite for operations requiring precise, controlled application of force rather than area suppression.","battleHistory":"~44"}},"name":"Nephilim Assault Battle Armor (Gauss)(Sqd6)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~45","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":16,"items":"~48","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"484":{"tro":{"production":"~41","text":{"overview":"~42","capabilities":"~49","deployment":"The Narc variant's target designation capability made it tactically valuable to Inner Sphere forces familiar with Narc-guided munitions. Four-trooper squads of salvaged Narc Nephilim could integrate into existing combined-arms formations, tagging enemy units for allied missile batteries. This was one of the few Demon configurations with clear utility beyond specialized Manei Domini operations.","battleHistory":"~44"}},"name":"Nephilim Assault Battle Armor (Narc)(Sqd4)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~45","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":16,"items":"~50","era":"jihad"},"485":{"tro":{"production":"~41","text":{"overview":"~42","capabilities":"~49","deployment":"Narc-equipped Nephilim troopers operated in six-trooper Level I formations as forward target designators within larger Blakist combined-arms formations. The Narc beacon allowed these Level Is to tag enemy units for guided missile strikes from allied launchers, while the support PPC and Gauss weapons provided enough personal firepower to hold their own if the engagement closed to direct combat range. This made them effective bridge units between reconnaissance and fire support roles.","battleHistory":"~44"}},"name":"Nephilim Assault Battle Armor (Narc)(Sqd6)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~45","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":16,"items":"~50","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"486":{"tro":{"production":"~41","text":{"overview":"~42","capabilities":"~51","deployment":"The Seeker configuration's enhanced sensor suite attracted significant intelligence interest from allied forces. Four-trooper squads assembled from captured examples contributed to post-war research into sensor-defeating countermeasures, as understanding the Seeker's detection capabilities helped develop tactics to counter similar threats in future conflicts.","battleHistory":"~44"}},"name":"Nephilim Assault Battle Armor (Seeker) [MG](Sqd4)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~45","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":16,"items":"~52","era":"jihad"},"487":{"tro":{"production":"~41","text":{"overview":"~42","capabilities":"~51","deployment":"No confirmed instances of Clan forces fielding Seeker-configured Nephilim suits in five-trooper Points have been documented. The hunter-killer role, while tactically useful, required the specialized sensor integration that was difficult to maintain without access to Blakist technical support infrastructure.","battleHistory":"~44"}},"name":"Nephilim Assault Battle Armor (Seeker) [MG](Sqd5)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~45","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":16,"items":"~52","squadSize":5,"era":"jihad"},"488":{"tro":{"production":"~41","text":{"overview":"~42","capabilities":"~51","deployment":"Seeker Nephilim troopers deployed in six-trooper Level I formations as dedicated hunter-killer teams. Their improved sensors and mimetic armor created operators who could detect enemies while remaining nearly invisible, an advantage the Manei Domini exploited ruthlessly. These Level Is were typically assigned high-value targets: enemy command vehicles, scout lances, and forward observation posts that needed to be eliminated quickly and quietly.","battleHistory":"~44"}},"name":"Nephilim Assault Battle Armor (Seeker) [MG](Sqd6)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~45","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":16,"items":"~52","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"489":{"tro":{"production":"~41","text":{"overview":"~42","capabilities":"~53","deployment":"The support Nephilim's fixed weapons and expendable rocket launchers made it relatively straightforward for Inner Sphere operators to field in four-trooper squads. The small laser's indefinite ammunition supply reduced logistical demands, and the fixed mounting simplified maintenance compared to the modular-armed variants. Small numbers may have seen limited post-Jihad evaluation service.","battleHistory":"~44"}},"name":"Nephilim Assault Battle Armor (Support)(Sqd4)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~45","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":16,"items":"~54","era":"jihad"},"490":{"tro":{"production":"~41","text":{"overview":"~42","capabilities":"~53","deployment":"Clan forces showed no documented interest in the support-configured Nephilim. The fixed small laser and expendable rockets offered no capabilities that Clan battle armor designs did not already provide at lower mass, and the suit's two-ton weight precluded OmniMech transport, a capability Clan warriors considered essential.","battleHistory":"~44"}},"name":"Nephilim Assault Battle Armor (Support)(Sqd5)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~45","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":16,"items":"~54","squadSize":5,"era":"jihad"},"491":{"tro":{"production":"~41","text":{"overview":"~42","capabilities":"~53","deployment":"Support Nephilim troopers operated in six-trooper Level I formations as combined-arms fire elements. The opening rocket volley followed by sustained laser fire gave these Level Is a reliable engagement profile that demanded less coordination than the modular variants. Domini commanders valued this configuration for operations where communications might be disrupted, as each trooper carried a self-contained weapons package that functioned independently of network support.","battleHistory":"~44"}},"name":"Nephilim Assault Battle Armor (Support)(Sqd6)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~45","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":16,"items":"~54","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"492":{"tro":{"production":"~55","text":{"overview":"~56","capabilities":"~57","deployment":"Following the reintroduction of the Nighthawk in 3065, Inner Sphere military commands organized their operators into standard four-trooper squads consistent with conventional infantry doctrine. These squads typically served as dedicated reconnaissance and intelligence-gathering elements attached to larger formations. The compact squad size proved well-suited to the Nighthawk's covert operational profile, with four-trooper teams small enough to infiltrate sensitive areas while still providing mutual support during extraction under fire.","battleHistory":"~58"}},"name":"Nighthawk PA(L) Mk. XXI(Sqd4)","weightClass":"pa-l","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~59","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":2,"items":"~60","era":"star-league"},"493":{"tro":{"production":"~55","text":{"overview":"~56","capabilities":"~57","deployment":"The original SLDF deployment of Nighthawks through the Special Forces Command occasionally employed five-trooper teams for operations requiring additional personnel or redundancy. This organizational pattern carried forward when Nighthawk technology accompanied the Exodus fleet, though the Clans ultimately developed their own battle armor rather than continuing the Nighthawk line. In rare instances where salvaged or captured Nighthawks have been fielded by Clan-affiliated forces, the familiar five-trooper Point structure has been applied.","battleHistory":"~58"}},"name":"Nighthawk PA(L) Mk. XXI(Sqd5)","weightClass":"pa-l","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~59","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":2,"items":"~60","squadSize":5,"era":"star-league"},"494":{"tro":{"production":"~55","text":{"overview":"~56","capabilities":"~57","deployment":"ComStar and the Word of Blake deployed Nighthawks in six-trooper Level I formations consistent with Com Guard organizational doctrine. ComStar's Fury Teams were among the first units to receive newly manufactured Mk. XXIs from the Tukayyid production facility, operating at full Level I strength for covert operations. The Word of Blake similarly organized their Nighthawk-equipped personnel into Level I formations, particularly within Protectorate Militia units where the suits supplemented conventional infantry capabilities.","battleHistory":"~58"}},"name":"Nighthawk PA(L) Mk. XXI(Sqd6)","weightClass":"pa-l","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~59","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":2,"items":"~60","squadSize":6,"era":"star-league"},"495":{"tro":{"production":"~61","text":{"overview":"~56","capabilities":"~62","deployment":"The Mk. XXX's enhanced ground speed makes four-trooper squads particularly effective for wide-area scouting missions where rapid overland movement is critical. Inner Sphere commands that have acquired these rare suits typically assign them to reconnaissance squads operating ahead of main force elements, leveraging the thirty kilometer per hour ground speed to cover far more territory than standard Nighthawk teams.","battleHistory":"~58"}},"name":"Nighthawk PA(L) Mk. XXX(Sqd4)","weightClass":"pa-l","groundMP":3,"motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~59","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":2,"era":"star-league"},"496":{"tro":{"production":"~61","text":{"overview":"~56","capabilities":"~62","deployment":"Five-trooper teams of the Mk. XXX exist primarily as an organizational option for forces using Clan-pattern formations. The variant's Star League origins and extreme rarity mean that Point-sized deployments have been exceedingly uncommon, limited to isolated instances where recovered suits were integrated into five-trooper teams following the operational practices of their parent command.","battleHistory":"~58"}},"name":"Nighthawk PA(L) Mk. XXX(Sqd5)","weightClass":"pa-l","groundMP":3,"motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~59","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":2,"squadSize":5,"era":"star-league"},"497":{"tro":{"production":"~61","text":{"overview":"~56","capabilities":"~62","deployment":"The limited number of Mk. XXX suits in circulation has restricted six-trooper Level I formations to rare occasions where a command possessed sufficient quantity. The enhanced ground mobility would provide a Level I with improved area coverage compared to standard Nighthawk formations, though the scarcity of the variant has prevented widespread adoption in ComStar or Word of Blake service.","battleHistory":"~58"}},"name":"Nighthawk PA(L) Mk. XXX(Sqd6)","weightClass":"pa-l","groundMP":3,"motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~59","armor":"ba-stealth-standard","armorPoints":2,"squadSize":6,"era":"star-league"},"498":{"tro":{"production":"~63","text":{"overview":"~64","capabilities":"~65","deployment":"Clan formations that acquired the Ogre organized Interdictor variants into five-trooper Points for specialized disruption roles. The ECM suite proved valuable in Clan combined-arms tactics, allowing Interdictor Points to screen advancing OmniMech formations while degrading enemy targeting and communications before heavier assets engaged.","battleHistory":"~66"}},"name":"Ogre Battle Armor (Interdictor)(Sqd5)","weightClass":"heavy","groundMP":2,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":14,"items":"~67","squadSize":5,"era":"early-rep"},"499":{"tro":{"production":"~63","text":{"overview":"~64","capabilities":"~65","deployment":"The six-trooper Interdictor formation extends the electronic warfare footprint of each tactical element, providing broader ECM coverage across a wider frontage. Like the standard configuration, this larger squad size saw limited fielding.","battleHistory":"~66"}},"name":"Ogre Battle Armor (Interdictor)(Sqd6)","weightClass":"heavy","groundMP":2,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":14,"items":"~67","squadSize":6,"era":"early-rep"},"500":{"tro":{"production":"~63","text":{"overview":"~64","capabilities":"~68","deployment":"Regulan planetary militias organized their Ogre troopers into four-member squads following Inner Sphere infantry doctrine. These squads typically operated alongside vehicle platoons, leveraging the suit's anti-armor capability to compensate for gaps in militia BattleMech strength. The four-trooper formation became the standard organizational template for all Inner Sphere Ogre operators.","battleHistory":"~66"}},"name":"Ogre Battle Armor (Sqd4)","weightClass":"heavy","groundMP":2,"arms":"~4","armorPoints":14,"items":"~69","era":"early-rep"},"501":{"tro":{"production":"~63","text":{"overview":"~64","capabilities":"~68","deployment":"Ogre suits acquired by the Rasalhague Dominion through Sea Fox merchants were organized into standard five-trooper Clan Points. Star Captain Idris DelVillar of the 283rd Battle Cluster employed his Ogre Points alongside Dasher OmniMechs, using the suit's bulk to exploit weak points in enemy formations. Initial Clan resistance to the Inner Sphere design was overcome after the Ogre proved itself in a Trial of Grievance.","battleHistory":"~66"}},"name":"Ogre Battle Armor (Sqd5)","weightClass":"heavy","groundMP":2,"arms":"~4","armorPoints":14,"items":"~69","squadSize":5,"era":"early-rep"},"502":{"tro":{"production":"~63","text":{"overview":"~64","capabilities":"~68","deployment":"The six-trooper formation provides additional firepower per tactical element, making it an option for organizations that structure their battle armor units around larger squad sizes. This configuration saw limited adoption compared to the four and five-trooper formations.","battleHistory":"~66"}},"name":"Ogre Battle Armor (Sqd6)","weightClass":"heavy","groundMP":2,"arms":"~4","armorPoints":14,"items":"~69","squadSize":6,"era":"early-rep"},"503":{"tro":{"production":"~70","text":{"overview":"~71","capabilities":"~72","deployment":"DCMS militia commands field the Bearhunter configuration in standard four-trooper squads, where the superheavy autocannon's anti-infantry firepower suits garrison duty well. These squads typically anchor defensive positions, screening vulnerable gun crews and support personnel. The Bearhunter's short-range lethality against lighter battle armor makes it a common Procurement Department assignment for units tasked with rear-area security.","battleHistory":"~73"}},"quirks":"~74","name":"Oni Battle Armor [Bearhunter](Sqd4)","mixedTech":true,"armorTechBase":"clan","groundMP":3,"arms":"~75","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":10,"items":"~76","era":"dark-ages"},"504":{"tro":{"production":"~70","text":{"overview":"~71","capabilities":"~72","deployment":"Combine formations influenced by Clan Nova Cat organizational doctrine occasionally fielded Oni troopers in five-member Points. The additional trooper increased the Bearhunter variant's close-range volume of fire, making these Points effective in defensive screening actions. This formation pattern saw limited adoption before the Nova Cats' destruction, and few Combine units maintained the practice afterward.","battleHistory":"~73"}},"quirks":"~74","name":"Oni Battle Armor [Bearhunter](Sqd5)","mixedTech":true,"armorTechBase":"clan","groundMP":3,"arms":"~75","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":10,"items":"~76","squadSize":5,"era":"dark-ages"},"505":{"tro":{"production":"~70","text":{"overview":"~71","capabilities":"~72","deployment":"Six-trooper formations of the Bearhunter configuration appeared in garrison commands that experimented with expanded squad structures modeled on ComStar's Level I organization. The larger formation concentrated more autocannon fire per tactical unit, though the increased logistical demands strained already difficult supply chains. Few commands sustained these oversized formations for extended periods.","battleHistory":"~73"}},"quirks":"~74","name":"Oni Battle Armor [Bearhunter](Sqd6)","mixedTech":true,"armorTechBase":"clan","groundMP":3,"arms":"~75","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":10,"items":"~76","squadSize":6,"era":"dark-ages"},"506":{"tro":{"production":"~70","text":{"overview":"~71","capabilities":"~77","deployment":"The medium recoilless rifle variant proved the most popular assignment among DCMS militia squads, offering four-trooper teams a flexible engagement profile suited to the unpredictable nature of garrison operations. During the fighting on Misery in 3139, Ryuken-go infantry commanders assigned Oni squads to back up field gun teams during a pincer action against the Tenth Avalon Hussars, where the suits performed well in a defensive screening role.","battleHistory":"~73"}},"quirks":"~74","name":"Oni Battle Armor [MRR](Sqd4)","mixedTech":true,"armorTechBase":"clan","groundMP":3,"arms":"~75","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":10,"items":"~78","era":"dark-ages"},"507":{"tro":{"production":"~70","text":{"overview":"~71","capabilities":"~77","deployment":"Five-trooper Points operating the recoilless rifle variant offered Clan-organized Combine formations a balanced direct-fire capability. The additional trooper provided useful redundancy for sustained engagements, as the medium recoilless rifle's ammunition consumption could leave individual troopers vulnerable during prolonged fighting. Combine units with Nova Cat heritage favored this configuration for its general-purpose reliability.","battleHistory":"~73"}},"quirks":"~74","name":"Oni Battle Armor [MRR](Sqd5)","mixedTech":true,"armorTechBase":"clan","groundMP":3,"arms":"~75","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":10,"items":"~78","squadSize":5,"era":"dark-ages"},"508":{"tro":{"production":"~70","text":{"overview":"~71","capabilities":"~77","deployment":"Expanded six-trooper formations carrying the medium recoilless rifle appeared in rear-echelon security units that adopted Level I organizational patterns. The larger squad provided sustained firepower over longer engagements, though quartermasters frequently noted the ammunition requirements of equipping six rifles per formation rather than the standard four.","battleHistory":"~73"}},"quirks":"~74","name":"Oni Battle Armor [MRR](Sqd6)","mixedTech":true,"armorTechBase":"clan","groundMP":3,"arms":"~75","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":10,"items":"~78","squadSize":6,"era":"dark-ages"},"509":{"tro":{"production":"~70","text":{"overview":"~71","capabilities":"~79","deployment":"The Compact Narc variant found a niche role among four-trooper militia squads operating alongside missile-equipped BattleMechs and vehicles. Effective employment demanded close coordination with supporting missile platforms, and commanders who invested in proper combined-arms training saw disproportionate returns from even a single Narc-equipped squad marking targets for friendly fire.","battleHistory":"~73"}},"quirks":"~74","name":"Oni Battle Armor [Narc](Sqd4)","mixedTech":true,"armorTechBase":"clan","groundMP":3,"arms":"~75","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":10,"items":"~80","era":"dark-ages"},"510":{"tro":{"production":"~70","text":{"overview":"~71","capabilities":"~79","deployment":"Five-trooper Points carrying the Compact Narc Launcher provided Clan-organized Combine formations with an organic target designation capability. The additional trooper improved survivability during the dangerous approach runs needed to place beacons, as the Point could suppress enemy positions while individual troopers closed to effective Narc range.","battleHistory":"~73"}},"quirks":"~74","name":"Oni Battle Armor [Narc](Sqd5)","mixedTech":true,"armorTechBase":"clan","groundMP":3,"arms":"~75","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":10,"items":"~80","squadSize":5,"era":"dark-ages"},"511":{"tro":{"production":"~70","text":{"overview":"~71","capabilities":"~79","deployment":"Six-trooper Narc formations were uncommon, as the designator role rarely benefited from additional personnel beyond what was needed for basic tactical security. Commands that deployed oversized Narc squads typically divided them into fire teams, with one group providing security while the other maneuvered to place beacons on priority targets.","battleHistory":"~73"}},"quirks":"~74","name":"Oni Battle Armor [Narc](Sqd6)","mixedTech":true,"armorTechBase":"clan","groundMP":3,"arms":"~75","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":10,"items":"~80","squadSize":6,"era":"dark-ages"},"512":{"tro":{"production":"~70","text":{"overview":"~71","capabilities":"~81","deployment":"Four-trooper squads armed with the Support PPC offered DCMS militia commanders a longer-range engagement option compared to the ballistic variants. These squads typically provided overwatch for defensive positions, engaging approaching threats before they closed to effective range. The PPC variant's limited ammunition supply meant careful fire discipline was essential, favoring experienced troopers over fresh recruits.","battleHistory":"~73"}},"quirks":"~74","name":"Oni Battle Armor [PPC](Sqd4)","mixedTech":true,"armorTechBase":"clan","groundMP":3,"arms":"~75","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":10,"items":"~82","era":"dark-ages"},"513":{"tro":{"production":"~70","text":{"overview":"~71","capabilities":"~81","deployment":"Five-trooper Points equipped with the energy weapon configuration provided Clan-organized Combine formations with concentrated long-range firepower. The additional trooper helped offset the Support PPC's limited ammunition by distributing fire across more weapons. This formation saw occasional use among units that valued ranged engagement over close-quarters fighting.","battleHistory":"~73"}},"quirks":"~74","name":"Oni Battle Armor [PPC](Sqd5)","mixedTech":true,"armorTechBase":"clan","groundMP":3,"arms":"~75","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":10,"items":"~82","squadSize":5,"era":"dark-ages"},"514":{"tro":{"production":"~70","text":{"overview":"~71","capabilities":"~81","deployment":"Six-trooper formations carrying the Support PPC were rare, as the energy weapon variant's higher training requirements made it difficult to fill oversized squads with qualified personnel. Commands that fielded these expanded formations typically deployed them in static defensive positions where the concentrated long-range fire justified the additional logistical overhead.","battleHistory":"~73"}},"quirks":"~74","name":"Oni Battle Armor [PPC](Sqd6)","mixedTech":true,"armorTechBase":"clan","groundMP":3,"arms":"~75","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":10,"items":"~82","squadSize":6,"era":"dark-ages"},"515":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Irian BattleMechs Unlimited|Irian BattleMechs Unlimited","primaryFactory":"Irian|Washburn"},"text":{"overview":"~83","capabilities":"The standard Phalanx configuration pairs a King David light gauss rifle with a detachable SRM-4 rack carrying an additional reload. The gauss rifle provides reliable long-range firepower, though its hitting power falls short of heavier man-portable weapons. The missile launcher compensates at closer ranges, and its detachable mounting allows troopers to jettison the empty pack when ammunition runs dry. A battle claw on the left arm enables OmniMech mounting, while an armored glove on the right permits handling of conventional infantry weapons and equipment requiring manual dexterity. This manipulator pairing sacrifices anti-BattleMech attack capability in favor of operational flexibility.\\n\\nGround speed reaches 22 km/h, adequate for keeping pace with most formations despite the suit’s lack of jump jets. Improved stealth armor reduces the sensor signature considerably, making the suit difficult to target at range. Protection is thin for the weight class, however, as concentrated energy fire can breach the armor in a single hit.","deployment":"Five-trooper formations operating the standard Phalanx configuration emerged among forces that adopted larger squad structures during the post-Jihad reorganization period. These teams leveraged the additional trooper to maintain sustained fire output over longer engagements, with the fifth member often carrying supplementary ammunition for the detachable missile packs.","battleHistory":"~84"}},"name":"Phalanx Battle Armor (A)(Sqd5)","weightClass":"heavy","groundMP":2,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-stealth-improved","armorPoints":7,"items":{"$k":"~86","$v":[["is-srm-4","body","#0",1],["is-king-david-light-gauss-rifle","ra","#1",null]]},"squadSize":5,"era":"civil-war"},"516":{"tro":{"production":{"manufacturer":"Free Worlds Defence Industries|Field Refit","primaryFactory":"Gibson|Various"},"text":{"overview":"~83","capabilities":"The Blakist Phalanx variant replaces the King David light gauss rifle with a ’Mech Taser Rifle, giving troopers the ability to temporarily disable vehicles and BattleMechs through electrical disruption. The taser’s one-shot capability makes target selection critical, as a missed opportunity leaves the trooper reliant on the remaining SRM-4 rack for heavy firepower. Additional electronic hardening protects the suit’s internal systems from the feedback that taser discharge can produce, though the weapon still carries a risk of catastrophic overload.\\n\\nThe remaining equipment mirrors the standard configuration: a battle claw and armored glove for OmniMech mounting and infantry weapon handling, improved stealth armor for reduced targeting signature, and 22 km/h ground speed without jump capability. The taser’s specialized nature limits the configuration’s general combat utility, making it a precision tool for disabling high-value targets rather than a frontline combat platform.","deployment":"Word of Blake Level I formations made extensive use of the Phalanx B during the Jihad, employing six-trooper teams for targeted strikes against enemy command vehicles and key BattleMechs. The larger formation allowed for coordinated taser employment, with multiple troopers cycling fire to maximize the chances of a successful disabling shot against priority targets.","battleHistory":"~84"}},"name":"Phalanx Battle Armor (B)(Sqd6)","weightClass":"heavy","groundMP":2,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-stealth-improved","armorPoints":7,"items":{"$k":"~86","$v":[["is-srm-4","body","#0",2],["is-taser","ra","#1",null]]},"squadSize":6,"era":"civil-war"},"517":{"tro":{"production":"~87","text":{"overview":"~83","capabilities":"~88","deployment":"Four-trooper squads fielding the Phalanx C provided organic indirect fire support to infantry formations that lacked conventional mortar or artillery assets. The heavy mortar’s ability to lob rounds over obstacles and into defilade positions gave squad-level commanders a capability previously reserved for dedicated support platoons.","battleHistory":"~84"}},"name":"Phalanx Battle Armor (C)(Sqd4)","weightClass":"heavy","groundMP":2,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-stealth-improved","armorPoints":7,"items":"~89","era":"jihad"},"518":{"tro":{"production":"~87","text":{"overview":"~83","capabilities":"~88","deployment":"Five-trooper formations operating the mortar-equipped Phalanx gained additional tactical flexibility through the extra team member, who could serve as a forward observer or security element while the remaining troopers focused on delivering indirect fire. The larger team also improved ammunition management during sustained bombardment operations.","battleHistory":"~84"}},"name":"Phalanx Battle Armor (C)(Sqd5)","weightClass":"heavy","groundMP":2,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-stealth-improved","armorPoints":7,"items":"~89","squadSize":5,"era":"jihad"},"519":{"tro":{"production":"~87","text":{"overview":"~83","capabilities":"~88","deployment":"Word of Blake Level I teams deployed the Phalanx C as a dedicated fire support element during the Jihad, with six-trooper formations providing sustained indirect bombardment in urban combat and siege operations. The mortar-equipped teams proved effective at dislodging defenders from fortified positions where direct assault would have been costly.","battleHistory":"~84"}},"name":"Phalanx Battle Armor (C)(Sqd6)","weightClass":"heavy","groundMP":2,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-stealth-improved","armorPoints":7,"items":"~89","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"520":{"tro":{"production":"~90","text":{"overview":"~83","capabilities":"~91","deployment":"Four-trooper squads operating the modernized Phalanx D found favor with garrison and patrol commands, where the energy weapon’s independence from ammunition supply chains reduced logistical strain on remote postings. The pulse laser’s adjustable fire modes gave squad leaders flexible engagement options for the varied threats encountered during peacekeeping operations.","battleHistory":"~84"}},"name":"Phalanx Battle Armor (D)(Sqd4)","weightClass":"heavy","groundMP":2,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-stealth-improved","armorPoints":7,"items":"~92","era":"dark-ages"},"521":{"tro":{"production":"~90","text":{"overview":"~83","capabilities":"~91","deployment":"Five-trooper formations equipped with the Phalanx D emerged as a preferred configuration for extended patrol and security operations, where the additional trooper expanded the team’s area coverage without increasing logistical demand. The energy-only armament proved particularly appealing for units operating far from established supply depots.","battleHistory":"~84"}},"name":"Phalanx Battle Armor (D)(Sqd5)","weightClass":"heavy","groundMP":2,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-stealth-improved","armorPoints":7,"items":"~92","squadSize":5,"era":"dark-ages"},"522":{"tro":{"production":"~90","text":{"overview":"~83","capabilities":"~91","deployment":"Six-trooper formations fielding the Phalanx D served in garrison and rapid-response roles, with the larger team size providing around-the-clock coverage for facility security and perimeter defense. The pulse laser’s sustained fire capability and low maintenance requirements made the configuration well suited for units maintaining long-term defensive postures.","battleHistory":"~84"}},"name":"Phalanx Battle Armor (D)(Sqd6)","weightClass":"heavy","groundMP":2,"arms":"~85","armor":"ba-stealth-improved","armorPoints":7,"items":"~92","squadSize":6,"era":"dark-ages"},"523":{"tro":{"production":"~93","text":{"overview":"~94","capabilities":"~95","deployment":"Four-trooper squads operating PowerLoaders became a common sight at Free Worlds League and Capellan Confederation commercial facilities, where the design first gained market traction. Inner Sphere docking operations favor the four-person squad structure, assigning each operator to a cargo handling lane within DropShip bays and warehouse loading areas. The PowerLoader's speed advantage over competing designs made it particularly popular in high-throughput commercial facilities.","battleHistory":"~96"}},"name":"PowerLoader Exoskeleton P-5000(Sqd4)","weightClass":"pa-l","groundMP":2,"arms":"~97","armorPoints":1,"items":"~98","era":"clan-inv"},"524":{"tro":{"production":"~93","text":{"overview":"~94","capabilities":"~95","deployment":"Clan merchant castes integrated the PowerLoader into their five-trooper Point formations after encountering the design through Inner Sphere trade. The suit's armor protection and faster movement speed aligned well with Clan efficiency standards, and Points of PowerLoader operators served cargo handling duties aboard Clan DropShips and at forward supply depots.","battleHistory":"~96"}},"name":"PowerLoader Exoskeleton P-5000(Sqd5)","weightClass":"pa-l","groundMP":2,"arms":"~97","armorPoints":1,"items":"~98","squadSize":5,"era":"clan-inv"},"525":{"tro":{"production":"~93","text":{"overview":"~94","capabilities":"~95","deployment":"ComStar and Word of Blake organized PowerLoader operators into six-trooper Level I teams for large-scale logistics operations. The suit's combination of speed and armor protection suited the demanding conditions at major HPG compound supply hubs, where operators handled continuous cargo flows under tight schedules. Level I formations could sustain higher work tempos than smaller squads, reducing the need for frequent crew rotations.","battleHistory":"~96"}},"name":"PowerLoader Exoskeleton P-5000(Sqd6)","weightClass":"pa-l","groundMP":2,"arms":"~97","armorPoints":1,"items":"~98","squadSize":6,"era":"clan-inv"},"526":{"tro":{"production":"~99","text":{"overview":"~100","capabilities":"~101","deployment":"The Republic Armed Forces adopted the Laser-equipped Purifier in four-trooper squads following the liberation of Terra, mirroring standard Inner Sphere infantry doctrine. Republic squads used the sustained-fire capability of the energy weapon to establish ambush positions along patrol routes and in urban environments, where the mimetic armor proved highly effective at concealing prepared defensive positions.","battleHistory":"~102"}},"name":"Purifier Adaptive Battle Armor [Laser](Sqd4)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~27","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":6,"items":"~103","era":"civil-war"},"527":{"tro":{"production":"~99","text":{"overview":"~100","capabilities":"~101","deployment":"Clan Points of five troopers operating Purifier Adaptive suits were uncommon, as the design originated outside Clan military tradition. Where Clan forces acquired Purifier suits through salvage or isorla, they organized operators into standard Points and favored the Laser configuration for its self-sufficiency and compatibility with Clan doctrine emphasizing independent small-unit action.","battleHistory":"~102"}},"name":"Purifier Adaptive Battle Armor [Laser](Sqd5)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~27","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":6,"items":"~103","squadSize":5,"era":"civil-war"},"528":{"tro":{"production":"~99","text":{"overview":"~100","capabilities":"~101","deployment":"Word of Blake Level I formations fielded six Laser-equipped Purifier troopers as standard reconnaissance and ambush teams. The extended-range energy weapon allowed Level I teams to engage targets from concealed positions indefinitely without ammunition concerns, making them ideal for sustained screening operations and perimeter defense around Blakist installations during the Jihad.","battleHistory":"~102"}},"name":"Purifier Adaptive Battle Armor [Laser](Sqd6)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~27","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":6,"items":"~103","squadSize":6,"era":"civil-war"},"529":{"tro":{"production":"~99","text":{"overview":"~100","capabilities":"~104","deployment":"Republic four-trooper squads equipped with the Narc configuration operated as forward observers for combined-arms formations, tagging enemy units with missile beacons to improve the accuracy of supporting fire. The smaller squad size proved adequate for the spotting role, though the loss of even one trooper during the vulnerable approach phase could compromise the entire mission.","battleHistory":"~102"}},"name":"Purifier Adaptive Battle Armor [Narc](Sqd4)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~27","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":6,"items":"~105","era":"civil-war"},"530":{"tro":{"production":"~99","text":{"overview":"~100","capabilities":"~104","deployment":"Clan salvage of Narc-configured Purifiers was rare, as the missile beacon system held limited appeal within Clan tactical doctrine that favored direct engagement over indirect support. Where five-trooper Points did operate the configuration, they typically employed it in conjunction with allied missile-equipped OmniMechs rather than the long-range indirect fire networks preferred by Inner Sphere commanders.","battleHistory":"~102"}},"name":"Purifier Adaptive Battle Armor [Narc](Sqd5)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~27","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":6,"items":"~105","squadSize":5,"era":"civil-war"},"531":{"tro":{"production":"~99","text":{"overview":"~100","capabilities":"~104","deployment":"Word of Blake Level I formations employed six-trooper Narc teams as dedicated missile spotting units operating ahead of Blakist combined-arms formations. The six-trooper team provided redundancy, ensuring that at least some troopers could close to tagging range even if others were detected during the approach. These teams proved effective at directing Jihad-era missile barrages against fortified positions.","battleHistory":"~102"}},"name":"Purifier Adaptive Battle Armor [Narc](Sqd6)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~27","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":6,"items":"~105","squadSize":6,"era":"civil-war"},"532":{"tro":{"production":"~99","text":{"overview":"~100","capabilities":"~106","deployment":"Republic four-trooper squads fielding the PPC configuration served as light sniper teams, exploiting the mimetic armor to establish concealed firing positions at range. The PPC variant entered service later than other configurations, and Republic commanders valued its standoff capability for area denial and harassment operations in the post-Jihad security environment.","battleHistory":"~102"}},"name":"Purifier Adaptive Battle Armor [PPC](Sqd4)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~27","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":6,"items":"~107","era":"jihad"},"533":{"tro":{"production":"~99","text":{"overview":"~100","capabilities":"~106","deployment":"Five-trooper Clan Points operating PPC-equipped Purifiers were exceedingly rare, as the infantry-scale weapon fell outside the typical Clan battle armor armament philosophy. Any Points so equipped likely resulted from battlefield salvage rather than deliberate procurement, with operators adapting to the weapon's sniping strengths on a case-by-case basis.","battleHistory":"~102"}},"name":"Purifier Adaptive Battle Armor [PPC](Sqd5)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~27","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":6,"items":"~107","squadSize":5,"era":"jihad"},"534":{"tro":{"production":"~99","text":{"overview":"~100","capabilities":"~106","deployment":"The Word of Blake introduced the PPC configuration to its Level I formations during the Jihad, adding a standoff sniping capability that complemented the mimetic armor. Six-trooper teams could establish overlapping fields of fire from concealed positions, engaging enemy forces at range while remaining nearly invisible. This configuration was particularly valued for defensive operations around critical Blakist facilities.","battleHistory":"~102"}},"name":"Purifier Adaptive Battle Armor [PPC](Sqd6)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~27","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":6,"items":"~107","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"535":{"tro":{"production":"~99","text":{"overview":"~100","capabilities":"~108","deployment":"Republic four-trooper squads equipped with TAG systems operated as artillery observers and missile designators within combined-arms task forces. The smaller squad size was sufficient for the spotting role, as TAG requires only one clear line of sight to a target. Republic commanders integrated these squads into defensive networks where concealed observation posts could direct devastating indirect fire.","battleHistory":"~102"}},"name":"Purifier Adaptive Battle Armor [TAG](Sqd4)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~27","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":6,"items":"~109","era":"civil-war"},"536":{"tro":{"production":"~99","text":{"overview":"~100","capabilities":"~108","deployment":"Clan Points employing TAG-equipped Purifiers were uncommon, though not unheard of among Clans that adopted combined-arms tactics incorporating artillery support. Five-trooper Points assigned to the spotting role operated in coordination with allied artillery Stars, using the suit's camouflage to maintain target designation without drawing counterfire.","battleHistory":"~102"}},"name":"Purifier Adaptive Battle Armor [TAG](Sqd5)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~27","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":6,"items":"~109","squadSize":5,"era":"civil-war"},"537":{"tro":{"production":"~99","text":{"overview":"~100","capabilities":"~108","deployment":"Word of Blake Level I formations fielded six TAG-equipped troopers as dedicated forward observers for Blakist artillery and missile assets. The six-trooper formation ensured continuous target designation capability even under fire, with multiple operators able to maintain locks on different targets simultaneously. These teams played a significant supporting role during siege operations throughout the Jihad.","battleHistory":"~102"}},"name":"Purifier Adaptive Battle Armor [TAG](Sqd6)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~27","armor":"ba-mimetic","armorPoints":6,"items":"~109","squadSize":6,"era":"civil-war"},"538":{"tro":{"production":"~110","text":{"overview":"~111","capabilities":"~112","deployment":"Inner Sphere military forces organize Quirinus troopers into four-trooper squads following standard infantry doctrine. Free Worlds League provincial militaries fielded David-equipped squads as long-range screening elements, using the rifle's reach and Light TAG capability to direct artillery strikes while maintaining safe standoff distances. Mercenary companies favored this configuration for reconnaissance contracts where engaging at range reduced casualties among their battle armor assets.","battleHistory":"~113"}},"name":"Quirinus Battle Armor [David](Sqd4)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~114","armor":"ba-reactive","armorPoints":8,"items":"~115","era":"early-rep"},"539":{"tro":{"production":"~110","text":{"overview":"~111","capabilities":"~112","deployment":"Organized into five-trooper Points, this formation reflects Clan organizational doctrine applied to the Quirinus chassis. Republic Armed Forces units with Clan-influenced command structures adopted the Point formation for battle armor operations, fielding David-equipped Points as forward spotting elements. The additional trooper provided greater survivability and allowed the Point to sustain effective TAG coordination even after taking casualties during extended engagements.","battleHistory":"~113"}},"name":"Quirinus Battle Armor [David](Sqd5)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~114","armor":"ba-reactive","armorPoints":8,"items":"~115","squadSize":5,"era":"early-rep"},"540":{"tro":{"production":"~110","text":{"overview":"~111","capabilities":"~112","deployment":"Six-trooper Level I formations follow the organizational heritage of the Com Guards, a structure retained by several Republic Armed Forces commands. David-equipped Level I units served as dedicated forward observer teams, pairing the rifle's long-range accuracy with concentrated TAG designation to direct massed indirect fire. The larger formation size allowed rotation of spotters, maintaining continuous target designation during prolonged bombardment operations.","battleHistory":"~113"}},"name":"Quirinus Battle Armor [David](Sqd6)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~114","armor":"ba-reactive","armorPoints":8,"items":"~115","squadSize":6,"era":"early-rep"},"541":{"tro":{"production":"~110","text":{"overview":"~111","capabilities":"~116","deployment":"Four-trooper squads equipped with the Heavy Grenade Launcher filled an area-suppression role within Inner Sphere infantry formations. Free Worlds League provincial forces deployed these squads in urban operations, where the grenade launcher's splash damage proved effective against fortified positions and clustered defenders. Mercenary units valued the configuration's flexibility, particularly during contracts requiring building clearance and close-quarters assault work.","battleHistory":"~113"}},"name":"Quirinus Battle Armor [GL](Sqd4)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~114","armor":"ba-reactive","armorPoints":8,"items":"~117","era":"early-rep"},"542":{"tro":{"production":"~110","text":{"overview":"~111","capabilities":"~116","deployment":"Five-trooper Points carrying Heavy Grenade Launchers operated as assault elements within Clan-structured formations adopted by certain Republic Armed Forces commands. The Point's additional trooper increased the volume of suppressive fire during advances, allowing grenade-equipped troopers to saturate defensive positions while their squadmates closed for battle claw attacks. This formation excelled in breaking through prepared infantry defenses.","battleHistory":"~113"}},"name":"Quirinus Battle Armor [GL](Sqd5)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~114","armor":"ba-reactive","armorPoints":8,"items":"~117","squadSize":5,"era":"early-rep"},"543":{"tro":{"production":"~110","text":{"overview":"~111","capabilities":"~116","deployment":"Level I formations equipped with Heavy Grenade Launchers served as dedicated assault teams within Republic Armed Forces units organized along Com Guard lines. The six-trooper formation delivered concentrated suppressive fire, overwhelming defensive positions through sheer volume of explosive ordnance. These units often operated in conjunction with BattleMech lances, softening entrenched infantry before heavier assets moved to exploit the breach.","battleHistory":"~113"}},"name":"Quirinus Battle Armor [GL](Sqd6)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~114","armor":"ba-reactive","armorPoints":8,"items":"~117","squadSize":6,"era":"early-rep"},"544":{"tro":{"production":"~110","text":{"overview":"~111","capabilities":"~118","deployment":"Four-trooper squads mounting Machine Guns served as anti-infantry specialists within Inner Sphere formations. Introduced later than the other configurations, machine gun squads found particular favor with mercenary companies operating in counterinsurgency environments where sustained suppressive fire against unarmored opponents was more valuable than anti-armor capability. Provincial garrison forces also adopted this loadout for base security and patrol operations.","battleHistory":"~113"}},"name":"Quirinus Battle Armor [MG](Sqd4)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~114","armor":"ba-reactive","armorPoints":8,"items":"~119","era":"dark-ages"},"545":{"tro":{"production":"~110","text":{"overview":"~111","capabilities":"~118","deployment":"Five-trooper Points equipped with Machine Guns provided anti-infantry screening within Clan-structured Republic Armed Forces formations. The Point's concentrated automatic fire could suppress large areas of open ground, making it effective for perimeter defense and convoy escort duties. The additional trooper compared to Inner Sphere squads allowed the Point to establish overlapping fields of fire across wider frontages.","battleHistory":"~113"}},"name":"Quirinus Battle Armor [MG](Sqd5)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~114","armor":"ba-reactive","armorPoints":8,"items":"~119","squadSize":5,"era":"dark-ages"},"546":{"tro":{"production":"~110","text":{"overview":"~111","capabilities":"~118","deployment":"Level I formations carrying Machine Guns operated as area-denial teams within Com Guard-derived Republic Armed Forces organizations. Six troopers delivering sustained automatic fire could control significant ground, making these formations valuable for securing critical infrastructure and denying movement corridors to hostile infantry. The larger formation absorbed casualties better during prolonged defensive operations while maintaining effective suppressive coverage.","battleHistory":"~113"}},"name":"Quirinus Battle Armor [MG](Sqd6)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~114","armor":"ba-reactive","armorPoints":8,"items":"~119","squadSize":6,"era":"dark-ages"},"547":{"tro":{"production":"~120","text":{"overview":"~121","capabilities":"~122","deployment":"The anti-infantry Raiden became a feared close-quarters weapon in DCMS urban warfare doctrine. Four-trooper squads equipped with flamers, machine guns, and vibro-claws led building clearance operations, with the combined weapon loadout allowing each trooper to engage multiple threat types without pausing to swap configurations. The variant first appeared as a field modification in 3058 before transitioning to factory production.","battleHistory":"~123"}},"name":"Raiden Battle Armor (Anti-Infantry)(Sqd4)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~124","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":9,"items":"~125","era":"clan-inv"},"548":{"tro":{"production":"~120","text":{"overview":"~121","capabilities":"~122","deployment":"Five-trooper Points of anti-infantry Raidens saw occasional deployment with Clan Nova Cat security formations operating in urban environments. The vibro-claw and dual weapon loadout suited the close-quarters fighting common in policing and pacification operations, where Clan formations needed anti-personnel capability beyond what standard Elemental configurations offered.","battleHistory":"~123"}},"name":"Raiden Battle Armor (Anti-Infantry)(Sqd5)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~124","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":9,"items":"~125","squadSize":5,"era":"clan-inv"},"549":{"tro":{"production":"~120","text":{"overview":"~121","capabilities":"~122","deployment":"Word of Blake Militia forces organized captured anti-infantry Raidens into six-trooper Level I formations during the Jihad. The variant's close-combat optimization made it effective for the brutal urban fighting that characterized Blakist occupation operations, where Level I teams of vibro-claw-equipped troopers cleared resistance strongholds building by building across contested Combine cities.","battleHistory":"~123"}},"name":"Raiden Battle Armor (Anti-Infantry)(Sqd6)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~124","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":9,"items":"~125","squadSize":6,"era":"clan-inv"},"550":{"tro":{"production":"~126","text":{"overview":"~121","capabilities":"~127","deployment":"The original Raiden prototype saw limited deployment in four-trooper squads with DCMS test units during 3052. These early evaluation teams provided critical feedback on the suit's performance characteristics before the Coordinator ordered its redesign, and surviving prototypes were eventually retired from frontline service once the production model became available.","battleHistory":"~123"}},"name":"Raiden Battle Armor (Original)(Sqd4)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~27","armorPoints":7,"items":"~128","era":"clan-inv"},"551":{"tro":{"production":"~126","text":{"overview":"~121","capabilities":"~127","deployment":"Clan Points operating captured Raiden prototypes were exceptionally rare, as very few of these early suits survived long enough to fall into enemy hands. Any Clan formation fielding five-trooper Points of original Raidens would have done so only briefly, treating the captured prototypes as intelligence assets to be studied rather than sustained combat equipment.","battleHistory":"~123"}},"name":"Raiden Battle Armor (Original)(Sqd5)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~27","armorPoints":7,"items":"~128","squadSize":5,"era":"clan-inv"},"552":{"tro":{"production":"~126","text":{"overview":"~121","capabilities":"~127","deployment":"ComStar's acquisition of original Raiden prototypes for evaluation within six-trooper Level I formations was limited to intelligence-gathering exercises. Com Guard analysts studied the prototype's polymer bonding techniques and SRM integration to inform their own battle armor procurement decisions, though the design's brief production run meant few examples were available.","battleHistory":"~123"}},"name":"Raiden Battle Armor (Original)(Sqd6)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~27","armorPoints":7,"items":"~128","squadSize":6,"era":"clan-inv"},"553":{"tro":{"production":"~120","text":{"overview":"~121","capabilities":"~129","deployment":"DCMS infantry squads favored the flamer-equipped Raiden for urban pacification and anti-insurgency operations, where four-trooper squads could clear buildings methodically. The flamer's psychological impact on conventional defenders often proved as effective as its thermal damage, with Combine commanders deploying these squads as shock elements during district-by-district sweeps.","battleHistory":"~123"}},"name":"Raiden Battle Armor [Flamer](Sqd4)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~22","armorPoints":9,"items":"~130","era":"clan-inv"},"554":{"tro":{"production":"~120","text":{"overview":"~121","capabilities":"~129","deployment":"Clan Nova Cat warriors organized captured and gifted Raiden suits into five-trooper Points following their alliance with the Draconis Combine. The flamer configuration saw use in Clan-style combined arms Stars, where a Point of flame-equipped Raidens could screen for heavier OmniMech assets during urban engagements along the Combine-Clan border regions.","battleHistory":"~123"}},"name":"Raiden Battle Armor [Flamer](Sqd5)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~22","armorPoints":9,"items":"~130","squadSize":5,"era":"clan-inv"},"555":{"tro":{"production":"~120","text":{"overview":"~121","capabilities":"~129","deployment":"Word of Blake Militia forces organized Raiden suits seized during the Jihad into six-trooper Level I formations. The flamer variant proved useful during the Blakist occupation of Combine worlds, where Level I teams conducted clearance operations against resistance cells sheltering in urban and industrial complexes.","battleHistory":"~123"}},"name":"Raiden Battle Armor [Flamer](Sqd6)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~22","armorPoints":9,"items":"~130","squadSize":6,"era":"clan-inv"},"556":{"tro":{"production":"~120","text":{"overview":"~121","capabilities":"~131","deployment":"The laser-equipped Raiden became the standard garrison configuration for DCMS armored infantry squads, with four-trooper teams assigned to planetary militia and second-line commands across the Combine. The weapon's independence from ammunition supply chains made it the preferred loadout for remote postings where resupply intervals stretched across months.","battleHistory":"~123"}},"name":"Raiden Battle Armor [Laser](Sqd4)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~22","armorPoints":9,"items":"~132","era":"clan-inv"},"557":{"tro":{"production":"~120","text":{"overview":"~121","capabilities":"~131","deployment":"Nova Cat garrison clusters occasionally fielded five-trooper Points of laser-armed Raidens alongside their own Clan-manufactured battle armor. The energy weapon's logistical simplicity appealed to Clan supply officers managing mixed-technology formations, and the configuration required no special ammunition procurement outside standard Clan supply channels.","battleHistory":"~123"}},"name":"Raiden Battle Armor [Laser](Sqd5)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~22","armorPoints":9,"items":"~132","squadSize":5,"era":"clan-inv"},"558":{"tro":{"production":"~120","text":{"overview":"~121","capabilities":"~131","deployment":"ComStar's Com Guards integrated laser-equipped Raidens into six-trooper Level I formations for garrison duty at HPG compounds throughout the Combine border. The ammunition-independent loadout reduced logistical demands on Com Guard supply networks, and the suit's heavy armor provided reliable protection for static defense assignments.","battleHistory":"~123"}},"name":"Raiden Battle Armor [Laser](Sqd6)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~22","armorPoints":9,"items":"~132","squadSize":6,"era":"clan-inv"},"559":{"tro":{"production":"~120","text":{"overview":"~121","capabilities":"~133","deployment":"Machine gun-equipped Raiden squads served extensively with DCMS infantry regiments tasked with anti-insurgency and border security operations. Four-trooper teams provided suppressive fire support for conventional infantry platoons, with the machine gun's high rate of fire proving decisive in engagements against unarmored irregular forces across the Combine's frontier worlds.","battleHistory":"~123"}},"name":"Raiden Battle Armor [MG](Sqd4)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~22","armorPoints":9,"items":"~134","era":"clan-inv"},"560":{"tro":{"production":"~120","text":{"overview":"~121","capabilities":"~133","deployment":"Five-trooper Points of machine gun Raidens appeared in mixed-equipment Clan Nova Cat garrison formations, where they supplemented heavier Clan battle armor in security and patrol roles. The ballistic weapon's effectiveness against conventional infantry made these Points useful for policing occupied territories alongside the Combine's shared border regions.","battleHistory":"~123"}},"name":"Raiden Battle Armor [MG](Sqd5)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~22","armorPoints":9,"items":"~134","squadSize":5,"era":"clan-inv"},"561":{"tro":{"production":"~120","text":{"overview":"~121","capabilities":"~133","deployment":"Word of Blake forces fielded machine gun Raidens in six-trooper Level I formations during occupation operations on conquered Combine worlds. The suppressive firepower of six machine gun-armed troopers operating as a coordinated team made these Level I units effective at controlling civilian populations and neutralizing lightly armed resistance fighters.","battleHistory":"~123"}},"name":"Raiden Battle Armor [MG](Sqd6)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~22","armorPoints":9,"items":"~134","squadSize":6,"era":"clan-inv"},"562":{"tro":{"production":"~120","text":{"overview":"~121","capabilities":"~135","deployment":"The MRM-equipped Raiden gave DCMS infantry squads a fire support capability that most medium battle armor lacked. Four-trooper squads armed with missile launchers operated in overwatch positions during combined arms operations, providing indirect harassment fire against enemy formations at ranges where conventional battle armor weapons could not reach.","battleHistory":"~123"}},"name":"Raiden Battle Armor [MRM](Sqd4)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~22","armorPoints":9,"items":"~136","era":"civil-war"},"563":{"tro":{"production":"~120","text":{"overview":"~121","capabilities":"~135","deployment":"Clan formations fielding five-trooper Points of MRM-armed Raidens used them as ranged skirmishers, exploiting the missile launcher's standoff capability to engage targets beyond the reach of typical battle armor weapons. These Points complemented close-combat Elemental formations by providing suppressive fire during approach maneuvers.","battleHistory":"~123"}},"name":"Raiden Battle Armor [MRM](Sqd5)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~22","armorPoints":9,"items":"~136","squadSize":5,"era":"civil-war"},"564":{"tro":{"production":"~120","text":{"overview":"~121","capabilities":"~135","deployment":"Six-trooper Level I formations equipped with MRM Raidens provided Blakist commanders with a concentrated volley of missile fire from a single infantry unit. The coordinated launch of six missiles per salvo, though individually modest, generated enough combined firepower to threaten light vehicles and disrupt enemy infantry formations at medium range.","battleHistory":"~123"}},"name":"Raiden Battle Armor [MRM](Sqd6)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~22","armorPoints":9,"items":"~136","squadSize":6,"era":"civil-war"},"565":{"tro":{"production":"~120","text":{"overview":"~121","capabilities":"~137","deployment":"DCMS squads armed with Tsunami-equipped Raidens filled an anti-armor niche within Combine infantry regiments. Four-trooper teams armed with heavy gauss rifles could threaten light vehicles and battle armor at ranges that forced opponents to respect the infantry's presence on the battlefield, a capability few other Inner Sphere medium battle armor designs matched.","battleHistory":"~123"}},"name":"Raiden Battle Armor [Tsunami](Sqd4)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~22","armorPoints":9,"items":"~138","era":"clan-inv"},"566":{"tro":{"production":"~120","text":{"overview":"~121","capabilities":"~137","deployment":"Five-trooper Points of Tsunami-armed Raidens gave Clan formations a dedicated anti-armor infantry element capable of engaging hardened targets at range. The heavy gauss rifle's stopping power complemented the close-combat orientation of standard Elemental Points, providing covering fire as heavier battle armor closed to engagement distance.","battleHistory":"~123"}},"name":"Raiden Battle Armor [Tsunami](Sqd5)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~22","armorPoints":9,"items":"~138","squadSize":5,"era":"clan-inv"},"567":{"tro":{"production":"~120","text":{"overview":"~121","capabilities":"~137","deployment":"Level I formations of Tsunami Raidens operated as dedicated anti-vehicle teams within Blakist and Com Guard infantry commands. Six heavy gauss rifles firing in coordination could damage or disable light combat vehicles, making these formations a credible deterrent against armored reconnaissance elements probing infantry-held positions.","battleHistory":"~123"}},"name":"Raiden Battle Armor [Tsunami](Sqd6)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~22","armorPoints":9,"items":"~138","squadSize":6,"era":"clan-inv"},"568":{"tro":{"production":"~139","text":{"overview":"~140","capabilities":"~141","deployment":"DCMS infantry squads received the Raiden II flamer configuration as a priority upgrade for frontline formations during the Dark Age. Four-trooper teams equipped with reflective armor proved particularly effective against opponents fielding energy-heavy battle armor and support vehicles, where the upgraded plating gave Combine troopers a decisive survivability advantage in close-range engagements.","battleHistory":"~142"}},"name":"Raiden II Battle Armor [Flamer](Sqd4)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~27","armor":"ba-laser-reflective","armorPoints":9,"items":"~143","era":"late-rep"},"569":{"tro":{"production":"~139","text":{"overview":"~140","capabilities":"~141","deployment":"Five-trooper Points of Raiden II suits appeared in DCMS formations that adopted Clan-influenced organizational structures following decades of cultural exchange. These Points operated within mixed battle armor Stars, where the reflective armor's energy resistance complemented heavier Clan-tech equipped units during combined arms operations along the Combine's contested borders.","battleHistory":"~142"}},"name":"Raiden II Battle Armor [Flamer](Sqd5)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~27","armor":"ba-laser-reflective","armorPoints":9,"items":"~143","squadSize":5,"era":"late-rep"},"570":{"tro":{"production":"~139","text":{"overview":"~140","capabilities":"~141","deployment":"Six-trooper formations of flamer-equipped Raiden II suits saw limited deployment with mercenary commands and allied contingents operating under DCMS contracts during the Dark Age. The larger formation size compensated for the reflective armor's reduced ballistic protection by concentrating more flamers per engagement, overwhelming defenders through volume of fire.","battleHistory":"~142"}},"name":"Raiden II Battle Armor [Flamer](Sqd6)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~27","armor":"ba-laser-reflective","armorPoints":9,"items":"~143","squadSize":6,"era":"late-rep"},"571":{"tro":{"production":"~139","text":{"overview":"~140","capabilities":"~144","deployment":"The heavy machine gun Raiden II became a preferred anti-infantry platform for DCMS four-trooper squads operating in contested urban zones during the Dark Age. The heavier caliber weapon gave Combine infantry squads greater hitting power against battle armor and light vehicles than the standard machine gun available to original Raiden formations.","battleHistory":"~142"}},"name":"Raiden II Battle Armor [HMG](Sqd4)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~27","armor":"ba-laser-reflective","armorPoints":9,"items":"~145","era":"late-rep"},"572":{"tro":{"production":"~139","text":{"overview":"~140","capabilities":"~144","deployment":"Five-trooper Points of HMG-armed Raiden II suits operated with DCMS commands that maintained Clan-style organizational patterns inherited from the long Nova Cat alliance. The heavy machine gun's ballistic punch offset the reflective armor's vulnerability to kinetic weapons in opposing forces, providing a balanced combat capability within mixed-equipment formations.","battleHistory":"~142"}},"name":"Raiden II Battle Armor [HMG](Sqd5)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~27","armor":"ba-laser-reflective","armorPoints":9,"items":"~145","squadSize":5,"era":"late-rep"},"573":{"tro":{"production":"~139","text":{"overview":"~140","capabilities":"~144","deployment":"Six-trooper formations of HMG Raiden II suits were fielded primarily by mercenary companies contracted to the Combine during the turbulent Dark Age period. The concentrated firepower of six heavy machine guns operating as a coordinated unit provided substantial anti-infantry and light anti-armor capability for commands that could not afford heavier dedicated assets.","battleHistory":"~142"}},"name":"Raiden II Battle Armor [HMG](Sqd6)","motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~27","armor":"ba-laser-reflective","armorPoints":9,"items":"~145","squadSize":6,"era":"late-rep"},"574":{"tro":{"production":"~146","text":{"overview":"~147","capabilities":"This configuration trades direct-fire punch for standoff engagement capability. The heavy recoilless rifle gives way to a lighter model, freeing mass for a three-tube long-range missile launcher fed by eight reloads. The rocket launchers are removed entirely. Additional armor plating brings protection to levels capable of withstanding simultaneous hits from both a particle cannon and a large laser, a significant improvement over the original design. The battle claw and ground speed remain unchanged from the base suit.\\n\\nThe missile launcher grants indirect fire capability that the standard configuration lacks, allowing troopers to engage targets from behind cover or contribute to massed volleys alongside conventional forces. The trade-off is reduced effectiveness at close range, where the light recoilless rifle cannot match the stopping power of its heavier predecessor.","deployment":"Inner Sphere commands deploy the missile-equipped variant in four-trooper squads optimized for fire support. These squads operate behind front-line formations, using indirect fire to soften targets before friendly forces close to engagement range. The configuration proved popular among mercenary units seeking affordable standoff capability without the logistical burden of heavier weapons platforms.","battleHistory":"~148"}},"name":"Ravager Assault Battle Armor (LRM)(Sqd4)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~22","armor":"ba-standard-advanced","armorPoints":18,"items":{"$k":"~86","$v":[["~149","ra","#0",null],["is-lrm-3","body","#1",8]]},"era":"late-rep"},"575":{"tro":{"production":"~146","text":{"overview":"~147","capabilities":"~150","deployment":"Inner Sphere militaries and mercenary commands organize Ravager operators into four-trooper squads following standard infantry doctrine. The suit's widespread availability on the open market made it a common sight among independent commands and smaller nations lacking domestic battle armor production. Squads frequently deploy in defensive roles, using the suit's heavy armor to anchor positions that lighter designs cannot hold.","battleHistory":"~148"}},"name":"Ravager Assault Battle Armor (Sqd4)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~22","armorPoints":18,"items":"~151","era":"jihad"},"576":{"tro":{"production":"~146","text":{"overview":"~147","capabilities":"~150","deployment":"Clan-affiliated forces that acquired the Ravager through commercial channels field it in traditional five-trooper Points. The Rasalhague Dominion and other Clan successor states adopted the suit where domestic production could not meet garrison demand. Points of Ravagers typically supplement second-line formations, with the suit's durability compensating for doctrinal preferences toward faster Clan designs.","battleHistory":"~148"}},"name":"Ravager Assault Battle Armor (Sqd5)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~22","armorPoints":18,"items":"~151","squadSize":5,"era":"jihad"},"577":{"tro":{"production":"~146","text":{"overview":"~147","capabilities":"~150","deployment":"Republic of the Sphere forces and ComStar remnant formations organize Ravager operators into six-trooper Level I units. The larger formation size allows these units to absorb casualties while maintaining combat effectiveness, a consideration well-suited to the Ravager's defensive orientation. Republic Armed Forces units fielded Ravagers extensively during the early Dark Age period as an affordable supplement to more advanced designs.","battleHistory":"~148"}},"name":"Ravager Assault Battle Armor (Sqd6)","weightClass":"assault","groundMP":2,"arms":"~22","armorPoints":18,"items":"~151","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"578":{"tro":{"production":"~152","text":{"overview":"~153","capabilities":"~154","deployment":"Inner Sphere forces that fielded the Interdictor variant organized operators into four-member squads. The ECM-equipped teams provided electronic countermeasures support for infantry formations operating in restrictive terrain where larger jamming platforms could not deploy effectively.","battleHistory":"~155"}},"name":"Resgate PA(L) (Interdictor)(Sqd4)","techBase":"clan","weightClass":"pa-l","groundMP":3,"motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~59","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":2,"items":"~156","era":"jihad"},"579":{"tro":{"production":"~152","text":{"overview":"~153","capabilities":"~154","deployment":"Clan Points of Interdictor-configured Resgates operated as electronic warfare screens for frontline formations. Five-operator Points provided sufficient ECM coverage to disrupt enemy targeting across a tactical zone, and the suit's jump mobility allowed rapid repositioning as the battle line shifted.","battleHistory":"~155"}},"name":"Resgate PA(L) (Interdictor)(Sqd5)","techBase":"clan","weightClass":"pa-l","groundMP":3,"motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~59","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":2,"items":"~156","squadSize":5,"era":"jihad"},"580":{"tro":{"production":"~152","text":{"overview":"~153","capabilities":"~154","deployment":"ComStar and Word of Blake Level I formations fielding the Interdictor configuration leveraged their six-operator strength to establish overlapping ECM coverage zones. The additional operators extended the effective disruption radius beyond what smaller formations could achieve in contested environments.","battleHistory":"~155"}},"name":"Resgate PA(L) (Interdictor)(Sqd6)","techBase":"clan","weightClass":"pa-l","groundMP":3,"motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~59","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":2,"items":"~156","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"581":{"tro":{"production":"~152","text":{"overview":"~153","capabilities":"~157","deployment":"Inner Sphere squads equipped with the Scout configuration served as forward reconnaissance elements for both military and emergency response operations. Four-member teams scouted disaster sites or forward areas to assess conditions and locate survivors before committing heavier assets.","battleHistory":"~155"}},"name":"Resgate PA(L) (Scout)(Sqd4)","techBase":"clan","weightClass":"pa-l","groundMP":3,"motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~59","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":2,"items":"~158","era":"jihad"},"582":{"tro":{"production":"~152","text":{"overview":"~153","capabilities":"~157","deployment":"Clan Points of Scout-equipped Resgates served dual roles as reconnaissance elements and forward search teams. The five-operator Point could sweep a wider area than smaller formations, making it effective for both military scouting and large-scale search operations across rugged terrain.","battleHistory":"~155"}},"name":"Resgate PA(L) (Scout)(Sqd5)","techBase":"clan","weightClass":"pa-l","groundMP":3,"motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~59","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":2,"items":"~158","squadSize":5,"era":"jihad"},"583":{"tro":{"production":"~152","text":{"overview":"~153","capabilities":"~157","deployment":"ComStar and Word of Blake Level I teams organized six Scout-configured operators for extended reconnaissance patrols. The larger formation size allowed continuous operations with rotating rest cycles, sustaining search efforts over longer periods than smaller units could maintain.","battleHistory":"~155"}},"name":"Resgate PA(L) (Scout)(Sqd6)","techBase":"clan","weightClass":"pa-l","groundMP":3,"motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~59","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":2,"items":"~158","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"584":{"tro":{"production":"~152","text":{"overview":"~153","capabilities":"~159","deployment":"Inner Sphere squads fielding the Spotter configuration provided forward target designation for supporting artillery and missile units. Four-operator teams typically operated in pairs, with one element designating targets while the other maintained security and backup designation capability.","battleHistory":"~155"}},"name":"Resgate PA(L) (Spotter)(Sqd4)","techBase":"clan","weightClass":"pa-l","groundMP":3,"motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~59","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":2,"items":"~160","era":"jihad"},"585":{"tro":{"production":"~152","text":{"overview":"~153","capabilities":"~159","deployment":"Clan Points of Spotter-configured Resgates operated as dedicated target acquisition elements within Stars. The five-operator formation allowed continuous designation capability with operators rotating through active spotting duty, ensuring uninterrupted fire support coordination during extended engagements.","battleHistory":"~155"}},"name":"Resgate PA(L) (Spotter)(Sqd5)","techBase":"clan","weightClass":"pa-l","groundMP":3,"motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~59","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":2,"items":"~160","squadSize":5,"era":"jihad"},"586":{"tro":{"production":"~152","text":{"overview":"~153","capabilities":"~159","deployment":"ComStar and Word of Blake Level I formations equipped with the Spotter configuration could maintain multiple simultaneous TAG designations. Six operators provided the redundancy needed to sustain targeting operations even when individual spotters were forced to reposition or temporarily lost contact with their assigned fire support elements.","battleHistory":"~155"}},"name":"Resgate PA(L) (Spotter)(Sqd6)","techBase":"clan","weightClass":"pa-l","groundMP":3,"motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~59","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":2,"items":"~160","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"587":{"tro":{"production":"~152","text":{"overview":"~153","capabilities":"~161","deployment":"Inner Sphere organizations that acquired Resgate exoskeletons organized their operators into four-member squads, consistent with standard infantry doctrine. These formations typically attached to larger emergency response units, where the smaller team size balanced area coverage with coordination. The four-operator squad proved adequate for most structural rescue scenarios encountered during peacetime disaster relief.","battleHistory":"~155"}},"name":"Resgate PA(L) (Sqd4)","techBase":"clan","weightClass":"pa-l","groundMP":3,"motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~16","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":2,"items":"~158","era":"clan-inv"},"588":{"tro":{"production":"~152","text":{"overview":"~153","capabilities":"~161","deployment":"Clan Cloud Cobra deployed Resgate operators in five-member Points, the standard Clan formation size. Points of Resgate operators served as dedicated emergency response elements within Cobra garrison clusters, tasked with disaster relief and personnel recovery across Cobra-held worlds. The five-trooper formation allowed broader search coverage during large-scale emergencies while maintaining the tight coordination expected of Clan units.","battleHistory":"~155"}},"name":"Resgate PA(L) (Sqd5)","techBase":"clan","weightClass":"pa-l","groundMP":3,"motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~16","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":2,"items":"~158","squadSize":5,"era":"clan-inv"},"589":{"tro":{"production":"~152","text":{"overview":"~153","capabilities":"~161","deployment":"ComStar and Word of Blake formations that obtained Resgate exoskeletons organized them into six-member Level I teams. These larger formations provided additional manpower for complex rescue operations, with the extra operators allowing simultaneous search and extraction efforts across wider disaster areas than smaller teams could manage.","battleHistory":"~155"}},"name":"Resgate PA(L) (Sqd6)","techBase":"clan","weightClass":"pa-l","groundMP":3,"motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~16","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":2,"items":"~158","squadSize":6,"era":"clan-inv"},"590":{"tro":{"production":"~152","text":{"overview":"~153","capabilities":"~162","deployment":"Inner Sphere squads equipped with the Support variant served as armed escorts for rescue teams operating in contested environments. Four-operator squads provided sufficient firepower to discourage opportunistic attacks on unarmed rescue exoskeletons without diverting heavier combat assets from frontline duties.","battleHistory":"~155"}},"name":"Resgate PA(L) (Support)(Sqd4)","techBase":"clan","weightClass":"pa-l","groundMP":3,"motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~59","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":2,"items":"~163","era":"jihad"},"591":{"tro":{"production":"~152","text":{"overview":"~153","capabilities":"~162","deployment":"Clan Points of Support-configured Resgates filled a security role within Cloud Cobra garrison formations. Five armed operators could establish a defensive perimeter around emergency operations sites, freeing heavier combat elements from static guard duty during disaster relief missions in contested territory.","battleHistory":"~155"}},"name":"Resgate PA(L) (Support)(Sqd5)","techBase":"clan","weightClass":"pa-l","groundMP":3,"motive":"jump","motiveMP":3,"arms":"~59","armor":"ba-fire-resistant","armorPoints":2,"items":"~163","squadSize":5,"era":"jihad"},"592":{"tro":{"production":"~164","text":{"overview":"~165","capabilities":"~166","deployment":"Inner Sphere-organized squads of Hybrid-equipped troopers specialized in reconnaissance and counter-insurgency operations within the Ghost Bear Dominion. Four-trooper stealth teams could infiltrate hostile areas with reduced detection signatures, gathering intelligence on pirate bands and political dissidents before withdrawing or calling in heavier elements to engage.","battleHistory":"~167"}},"quirks":"~168","name":"Rogue Bear Heavy Battle Armor (Hybrid)(Sqd4)","techBase":"clan","mixedTech":true,"weightClass":"heavy","motive":"jump","motiveMP":2,"arms":"~169","armor":"ba-stealth-basic","armorPoints":10,"items":"~170","apMounts":"~171","era":"jihad"},"593":{"tro":{"production":"~164","text":{"overview":"~165","capabilities":"~166","deployment":"Clan Points of Hybrid Rogue Bears operated primarily within second-line Clusters that valued reconnaissance capability. The stealth armor and camouflage systems allowed these Points to conduct scouting operations that traditional Clan doctrine assigned to faster light OmniMechs, filling a gap in formations that lacked sufficient aerospace or light BattleMech assets for long-range patrol duties.","battleHistory":"~167"}},"quirks":"~168","name":"Rogue Bear Heavy Battle Armor (Hybrid)(Sqd5)","techBase":"clan","mixedTech":true,"weightClass":"heavy","motive":"jump","motiveMP":2,"arms":"~169","armor":"ba-stealth-basic","armorPoints":10,"items":"~170","apMounts":"~171","squadSize":5,"era":"jihad"},"594":{"tro":{"production":"~164","text":{"overview":"~165","capabilities":"~166","deployment":"Six-trooper Hybrid formations offered expanded reconnaissance coverage per tactical element, with additional troopers extending the observation capacity of each deployed team. The larger formation size partially offset the Hybrid's lighter armament by providing more machine guns and anti-personnel weapons per engagement, though stealth benefits diminished somewhat with the larger thermal and electromagnetic signature of six active suits.","battleHistory":"~167"}},"quirks":"~168","name":"Rogue Bear Heavy Battle Armor (Hybrid)(Sqd6)","techBase":"clan","mixedTech":true,"weightClass":"heavy","motive":"jump","motiveMP":2,"arms":"~169","armor":"ba-stealth-basic","armorPoints":10,"items":"~170","apMounts":"~171","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"595":{"tro":{"production":"~164","text":{"overview":"~165","capabilities":"~172","deployment":"KungsArmé formations within the Ghost Bear Dominion organized Rogue Bear troopers into four-member squads, following the Inner Sphere infantry doctrine that many Rasalhagian warriors retained after absorption into the touman. These squads proved effective during the brutal fighting against Word of Blake forces in the Draconis Combine, where four-trooper teams could be integrated into existing Rasalhagian platoon structures without requiring full Clan-style reorganization.","battleHistory":"~167"}},"name":"Rogue Bear Heavy Battle Armor (Sqd4)","techBase":"clan","weightClass":"heavy","motive":"jump","motiveMP":2,"arms":"~173","armorPoints":13,"items":"~174","era":"jihad"},"596":{"tro":{"production":"~164","text":{"overview":"~165","capabilities":"~172","deployment":"Clan Ghost Bear deployed Rogue Bear troopers in standard five-warrior Points, the foundational tactical unit of Clan military organization. Garrison Clusters across the Dominion fielded Rogue Bear Points as rapid-response elements, their jump capability and fixed armament well-suited to the patrol and security duties that defined second-line operations. The 283rd Battle Cluster on Mannedorf maintained particularly strong Rogue Bear contingents given its proximity to the production facility.","battleHistory":"~167"}},"name":"Rogue Bear Heavy Battle Armor (Sqd5)","techBase":"clan","weightClass":"heavy","motive":"jump","motiveMP":2,"arms":"~173","armorPoints":13,"items":"~174","squadSize":5,"era":"jihad"},"597":{"tro":{"production":"~164","text":{"overview":"~165","capabilities":"~172","deployment":"Select formations within the Ghost Bear Dominion organized Rogue Bear troopers into six-member tactical units, a structure associated with ComStar-derived military doctrine. This larger formation size provided additional firepower density per tactical element at the cost of reduced flexibility, favoring defensive operations and fixed-position security assignments where concentrated missile and machine gun fire could cover wider engagement zones.","battleHistory":"~167"}},"name":"Rogue Bear Heavy Battle Armor (Sqd6)","techBase":"clan","weightClass":"heavy","motive":"jump","motiveMP":2,"arms":"~173","armorPoints":13,"items":"~174","squadSize":6,"era":"jihad"},"598":{"tro":{"production":"~164","text":{"overview":"~165","capabilities":"~175","deployment":"Rasalhague Dominion units maintaining Inner Sphere organizational structures fielded the Upgrade in four-trooper squads during the Dark Age. The reactive armor configuration proved well-suited to the squad-level close-quarters engagements that characterized anti-piracy and border security operations, where four-trooper teams could hold chokepoints and absorb return fire more effectively than the original design allowed.","battleHistory":"~167"}},"name":"Rogue Bear Heavy Battle Armor (Upgrade)(Sqd4)","techBase":"clan","weightClass":"heavy","motive":"jump","motiveMP":2,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-reactive","armorPoints":13,"items":"~176","era":"late-rep"},"599":{"tro":{"production":"~164","text":{"overview":"~165","capabilities":"~175","deployment":"Clan Points of Upgrade-equipped Rogue Bears served across Rasalhague Dominion garrison Clusters during the Dark Age. The reactive armor and heavy battle claws shifted the suit's tactical profile toward a more defensive posture, and Point commanders adapted their tactics accordingly, favoring ambush positions and prepared fighting positions over the aggressive assault doctrine typical of standard Rogue Bear Points.","battleHistory":"~167"}},"name":"Rogue Bear Heavy Battle Armor (Upgrade)(Sqd5)","techBase":"clan","weightClass":"heavy","motive":"jump","motiveMP":2,"arms":"~4","armor":"ba-reactive","armorPoints":13,"items":"~176","squadSize":5,"era":"late-rep"}}`)};export{e as default};